During the Reagan years, the federal war on drugs was significantly ramped up, and a number of states acted to increase penalties for drug crimes.<ref name="Dufton" /><ref name="gerber" /><ref name="Lee" />  Meanwhile, NORML struggled to regain the influence that it once held, as it dealt with severe decreases in funding and membership, and underwent frequent changes to organizational leadership.<ref name="Dufton" />  In 1985 part of NORML was split off to found the Drug Policy Foundation, which was then merged with the [[Lindesmith Center]] to become the [[Drug Policy Alliance]] in 2000.<ref name="Heddleston" />  Members of NORML further split off in 1995 to found the [[Marijuana Policy Project]].

In 1990, Alaska voters approved a ballot initiative to recriminalize cannabis, overriding the court decision that legalized cannabis 15 years earlier.<ref>{{cite news|title=Alaska Voters Approve Measure To Recriminalize Marijuana|url=https://apnews.com/article/4db1bf97a3d7c3a94c1d1e304f149c06|access-date=January 19, 2017|work=Associated Press News|date=November 7, 1990}}</ref>  Also in 1990, the [[Solomon–Lautenberg amendment]] was enacted at the federal level, leading many states to further criminalize cannabis by passing "Smoke a joint, lose your license" laws.<ref>{{citation |title="Possess a Joint, Lose Your License": July 1995 Status Report |url=http://www.mpp.org/site/c.glKZLeMQIsG/b.1087547/k.33C1/Possess_a_Joint_Lose_Your_License.htm |publisher=Marijuana Policy Project |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20071008235035/http://www.mpp.org/site/c.glKZLeMQIsG/b.1087547/k.33C1/Possess_a_Joint_Lose_Your_License.htm |archive-date=October 8, 2007 }}</ref><ref>{{cite news|title=States Are Pressed to Suspend Driver Licenses of Drug Users|url=https://www.nytimes.com/1990/11/16/us/states-are-pressed-to-suspend-driver-licenses-of-drug-users.html|access-date=January 28, 2018|work=The New York Times|agency=Associated Press|date=November 16, 1990}}</ref>  These laws imposed mandatory driver's license suspensions of at least six months for committing any type of drug offense (regardless of whether any motor vehicle was involved) including the simple possession of cannabis.<ref>{{cite web|title=Reefer sanity: States abandon driver's license suspensions for drug offenses|url=http://clemencyreport.org/drivers-license-suspensions-drug-offenses-state-state-list/|website=The Clemency Report|access-date=January 19, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180119234957/http://clemencyreport.org/drivers-license-suspensions-drug-offenses-state-state-list/|archive-date=January 19, 2018|url-status=unfit}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|last1=Beitsch|first1=Rebecca|title=States Reconsider Driver's License Suspensions for People With Drug Convictions|url=http://www.pewtrusts.org/en/research-and-analysis/blogs/stateline/2017/01/31/states-reconsider-drivers-license-suspensions-for-people-with-drug-convictions|access-date=January 19, 2018|work=Stateline|publisher=The Pew Charitable Trusts|date=January 31, 2017}}</ref>  As of 2021 only three states (Alabama, Arkansas, and Florida) continue to have such laws in effect.<ref>{{cite news|last1=Aiken|first1=Joshua|title=Reinstating Common Sense: How driver's license suspensions for drug offenses unrelated to driving are falling out of favor|url=https://www.prisonpolicy.org/driving/national.html|access-date=January 19, 2018|work=Prison Policy Initiative|date=December 12, 2016}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|title=U.S. Representative Beto O'Rourke Leads Bipartisan Bill that Repeals Federal Transportation Law Requiring States to Suspend Driver's Licenses for Drug Offenses|url=http://www.drugpolicy.org/news/2017/04/us-representative-beto-orourke-leads-bipartisan-bill-repeals-federal-transportation-law|access-date=January 19, 2018|work=Drug Policy Alliance|date=April 5, 2017|archive-date=May 19, 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230519101059/https://drugpolicy.org/news/2017/04/us-representative-beto-orourke-leads-bipartisan-bill-repeals-federal-transportation-law}}</ref>

===Second wave of decriminalization begins (2001)===
After Nebraska decriminalized cannabis in 1978, no other state would follow for over two decades, until [[Nevada]] decriminalized in 2001.<ref>{{cite news|title=Nevada Defelonizes Pot Possession – State Eliminates Jail, Criminal Record for Minor Offenders; Legalizes Medical Marijuana for Seriously Ill|url=http://norml.org/news/2001/06/07/nevada-defelonizes-pot-possessionstate-eliminates-jail-criminal-record-for-minor-offenders-legalizes-medical-marijuana-for-seriously-ill|access-date=February 9, 2018|work=NORML|date=June 7, 2001}}</ref>  In subsequent years a number of major cities decriminalized cannabis or made enforcement of cannabis laws the lowest priority.<ref>{{cite web |title=Executive Summary |url=https://norml.org/laws/local-decriminalization/executive-summary/ |website=NORML |access-date=December 7, 2012}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|last1=Bloom|first1=Steve|title=States Where Marijuana Is Legalized, Decriminalized or Medicalized|url=https://www.celebstoner.com/news/marijuana-news/2017/04/26/marijuana-laws-nationwide/|access-date=May 23, 2021|work=Celeb Stoner|date=November 8, 2017}}</ref>  Among the first major cities to pass such measures were [[Seattle]] (2003),<ref>{{cite news|title=Seattle Voters Approve Initiative Making Marijuana Enforcement City's "Lowest Priority"|url=http://norml.org/news/2003/09/18/seattle-voters-approve-initiative-making-marijuana-enforcement-city-s-lowest-priority|access-date=January 20, 2018|work=NORML|date=September 18, 2003}}</ref> [[Oakland, California|Oakland]] (2004),<ref>{{cite news|title=Voters Nationwide Embrace Marijuana Law Reform Proposals|url=http://norml.org/news/2004/11/03/voters-nationwide-embrace-marijuana-law-reform-proposals|access-date=January 20, 2018|work=NORML|date=November 3, 2004}}</ref> [[Denver]] (2005),<ref>{{cite news|title=Denver Residents Legalize Marijuana Possession|url=https://www.foxnews.com/story/denver-residents-legalize-marijuana-possession|access-date=January 20, 2018|work=Fox News|agency=Associated Press|date=November 2, 2005}}</ref> and [[San Francisco]] (2006).<ref>{{cite news|title=San Francisco Board of Supervisors Vote Overwhelmingly to Deprioritize Adult Marijuana Offenses; Now Officially Lowest Law Enforcement Priority|url=http://www.drugpolicy.org/news/2006/11/san-francisco-board-supervisors-vote-overwhelmingly-deprioritize-adult-marijuana-offens|access-date=January 20, 2018|work=Drug Policy Alliance|date=November 14, 2006|archive-date=January 21, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180121184309/http://www.drugpolicy.org/news/2006/11/san-francisco-board-supervisors-vote-overwhelmingly-deprioritize-adult-marijuana-offens}}</ref>  In the years that followed reform efforts continued to gain steam, with decriminalization laws passing in [[Massachusetts]] (2008),<ref>{{cite news|last1=Abel|first1=David|title=Voters approve marijuana law change|url=https://archive.boston.com/news/local/articles/2008/11/05/voters_approve_marijuana_law_change/|access-date=January 19, 2018|work=The Boston Globe|date=November 5, 2008}}</ref> [[Connecticut]] (2011),<ref>{{cite news |last1=Armentano |first1=Paul |title=Connecticut: Marijuana Decriminalization Measure Is Now Law |url=https://blog.norml.org/2011/07/05/connecticut-marijuana-decriminalization-measure-now-law/ |access-date=November 10, 2018 |work=NORML |date=July 5, 2011}}</ref> [[Rhode Island]] (2012),<ref>{{cite news|title=Rhode Island: Marijuana Decriminalization Measure Signed Into Law|url=http://norml.org/news/2012/06/21/rhode-island-marijuana-decriminalization-measure-signed-into-law|access-date=January 19, 2018|work=NORML|date=June 21, 2012}}</ref> [[Vermont]] (2013),<ref>{{cite news|last1=Wing|first1=Nick|title=Vermont Marijuana Decriminalization Signed Into Law, Reduces Penalties For Possession Up To An Ounce|url=https://www.huffingtonpost.com/2013/06/06/vermont-marijuana-decriminalization_n_3397266.html|access-date=February 9, 2018|work=HuffPost|date=June 6, 2013}}</ref> the [[Washington, D.C.|District of Columbia]] (2014),<ref>{{cite news |last1=Simpson |first1=Ian |title=Washington mayor signs marijuana decriminalization bill |url=https://www.reuters.com/article/us-usa-marijuana-districtofcolumbia/washington-mayor-signs-marijuana-decriminalization-bill-idUSBREA2U1OK20140331 |access-date=November 10, 2014 |work=Reuters |date=March 31, 2014}}</ref> [[Maryland]] (2014),<ref>{{cite news|last1=Reilly|first1=Molly|title=Maryland Decriminalizes Marijuana Possession|url=https://www.huffingtonpost.com/2014/04/14/maryland-marijuana-decriminalization_n_5107412.html|access-date=January 19, 2018|work=HuffPost|date=April 15, 2014}}</ref> [[Missouri]] (2014),<ref>{{cite news|title=Missouri Becomes 19th State To Decriminalize Marijuana Possession|url=https://blog.mpp.org/prohibition/missouri-becomes-19th-state-to-decriminalize-marijuana-possession/|access-date=January 19, 2018|work=Marijuana Policy Project|date=May 16, 2014}}</ref> the [[U.S. Virgin Islands]] (2014),<ref>{{cite news|last1=Armento|first1=Paul|title=Pot Possession Decriminalized In US Virgin Islands|url=http://blog.norml.org/2014/12/22/pot-possession-decriminalized-in-us-virgin-islands/|access-date=January 19, 2018|work=NORML|date=December 22, 2014}}</ref> [[Delaware]] (2015),<ref>{{cite news|last1=Starkey|first1=Jonathan|title=Marijuana decriminalization bill OK'd in Delaware|url=https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/nation/2015/06/18/marijuana-decriminalization-bill-okd-delaware/28955055/|access-date=January 19, 2018|work=USA Today|date=June 18, 2015}}</ref> [[Illinois]] (2016),<ref>{{cite news|last1=Monique|first1=Garcia|title=Rauner reduces punishment for minor pot possession from jail to citation|url=http://www.chicagotribune.com/news/local/breaking/ct-illinois-marijuana-decriminalzation-0730-20160729-story.html|access-date=January 19, 2018|work=Chicago Tribune|date=July 29, 2016}}</ref> [[New Hampshire]] (2017),<ref>{{cite news|last1=Wilson|first1=Reid|title=NH governor signs marijuana decriminalization bill|url=https://thehill.com/homenews/state-watch/342692-nh-governor-signs-marijuana-decriminalization-bill/|access-date=January 19, 2018|work=The Hill|date=July 19, 2017}}</ref> [[New Mexico]] (2019),<ref>{{cite news |last1=Angell |first1=Tom |title=New Mexico Governor Signs Marijuana Decriminalization Bill |url=https://www.forbes.com/sites/tomangell/2019/04/04/new-mexico-governor-signs-marijuana-decriminalization-bill/ |access-date=April 4, 2019 |work=Forbes |date=April 4, 2019}}</ref> [[North Dakota]] (2019),<ref>{{cite news |last1=Lopez |first1=German |title=North Dakota quietly decriminalized marijuana |url=https://www.vox.com/policy-and-politics/2019/5/10/18563776/north-dakota-marijuana-decriminalization-legalization |access-date=May 11, 2019 |work=Vox |date=May 10, 2019}}</ref> [[Hawaii]] (2019),<ref>{{cite news |last1=Angell |first1=Tom |title=Hawaii Marijuana Decriminalization Will Take Effect, Governor Says |url=https://www.marijuanamoment.net/hawaii-marijuana-decriminalization-will-take-effect-governor-says/ |access-date=June 27, 2019 |work=Marijuana Moment |date=June 25, 2019}}</ref> [[Virginia]] (2020),<ref>{{cite news |last1=Jaeger |first1=Kyle |title=Virginia Governor Approves Marijuana Decriminalization Bill |url=https://www.marijuanamoment.net/virginia-governor-approves-marijuana-decriminalization-bill/ |access-date=April 12, 2020 |work=Marijuana Moment |date=April 12, 2020}}</ref> and [[Louisiana]] (2021).<ref>{{cite news |last1=Karlin |first1=Sam |title=John Bel Edwards signs bill decriminalizing marijuana, says it's not decriminalization |url=https://www.theadvocate.com/baton_rouge/news/politics/legislature/article_fe7ab00a-cdfc-11eb-95f1-87bbdda37b6b.html |access-date=June 15, 2021 |work=The Advocate |date=June 15, 2021}}</ref>  As of 2023 seven states have decriminalization policies in effect, and an additional 19 states have decriminalized that later legalized.

===State recreational legalization begins (2012)===
{{See also|Green rush}}
Prior to 2012, [[Initiatives and referendums in the United States|ballot initiatives]] seeking to legalize cannabis were attempted in a number of states but none succeeded.  Among these attempts were California in 1972 (33% support), Oregon in 1986 (26%), Alaska in 2000 (41%), Nevada in 2002 (39%), Alaska in 2004 (44%), Colorado in 2006 (46%), Nevada in 2006 (46%), and California in 2010 (47%).<ref name="ballotpedia">{{cite web|title=History of marijuana on the ballot|url=https://ballotpedia.org/History_of_marijuana_on_the_ballot|website=Ballotpedia|access-date=January 21, 2018}}</ref>

In 2012, success was finally achieved for legalization advocates in the states of [[Washington (state)|Washington]] and [[Colorado]], when voters approved [[Initiative 502]] and [[Amendment 64]].<ref name="ballotpedia" />  In subsequent years, cannabis was legalized by ballot measure in [[Oregon]] (2014), [[Alaska]] (2014), the [[Washington, D.C.|District of Columbia]] (2014), [[California]] (2016), [[Nevada]] (2016), [[Maine]] (2016), [[Massachusetts]] (2016), [[Michigan]] (2018), [[Arizona]] (2020), [[Montana]] (2020), [[New Jersey]] (2020), [[Maryland]] (2022), [[Missouri]] (2022), and [[Ohio]] (2023), and by an act of legislature in [[Vermont]] (2018), the [[Northern Mariana Islands]] (2018), [[Guam]] (2019), [[Illinois]] (2019), [[New York (state)|New York]] (2021), [[Virginia]] (2021), [[New Mexico]] (2021), [[Connecticut]] (2021), [[Rhode Island]] (2022), the [[United States Virgin Islands|U.S. Virgin Islands]] (2023), [[Delaware]] (2023), and [[Minnesota]] (2023).<ref name="ncsl" />{{efn|[[South Dakota]] voters approved a ballot measure to legalize cannabis in November 2020, but it was overturned in a February 2021 court ruling.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Groves |first1=Stephen |title=South Dakota judge rejects amendment legalizing marijuana |url=https://apnews.com/article/constitutions-south-dakota-recreational-marijuana-marijuana-kristi-noem-c990e08307ca5016d789f7504cb6e7e6 |access-date=May 21, 2021 |work=Associated Press |date=February 8, 2021}}</ref>  The case remains under appeal to the South Dakota Supreme Court.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Groves |first1=Stephen |title=South Dakota Supreme Court weighs pot legalization battle |url=https://apnews.com/article/constitutions-medical-marijuana-south-dakota-marijuana-kristi-noem-3888b3480448a4ca7bf71f7d6e455733 |access-date=May 21, 2021 |work=Associated Press |date=April 28, 2021}}</ref>}}  In all of these jurisdictions, commercial distribution has been legalized except in Virginia and the District of Columbia, personal cultivation has been legalized except in Delaware, Illinois, New Jersey, and Washington state,{{efn|Illinois and Washington state only allow cultivation for medical cannabis patients.<ref>{{cite web|title=Washington State's New Cannabis Homegrow Rules|author=Daniel Shortt |date=August 3, 2017|work=Canna Law blog|publisher=Harris Bricken PLLC|url=https://www.cannalawblog.com/washington-states-new-cannabis-homegrow-rules/}}</ref>}} public consumption is prohibited except in New York (though on-premises consumption is allowed in some jurisdictions),<ref>{{cite news |last1=Ferré-Sadurní |first1=Luis |last2=Closson |first2=Troy |title=New York Has Legalized Marijuana. Here's What to Know. |url=https://www.nytimes.com/article/new-york-marijuana-legalization-facts.html |access-date=April 9, 2021 |work=The New York Times |date=April 1, 2021}}</ref> and use by individuals under 21 years old is prohibited.<ref>{{cite web |title=Legal Recreational Marijuana States and DC |url=https://marijuana.procon.org/view.resource.php?resourceID=006868 |website=ProCon.org |access-date=May 2, 2019 |date=November 7, 2018}}</ref>

===Federal policy post-2012===
After the first states legalized in 2012, uncertainty remained over how the federal government would respond.  Seeking to clarify, the Justice Department issued the [[Cole Memorandum]] in August 2013, which specified eight conditions under which enforcement of federal law would be prioritized (such as distribution of cannabis to minors or diversion across state borders).<ref name="cole memo">{{cite news|last1=Rough|first1=Lisa|title=The Cole Memo: What Is It and What Does It Mean?|url=https://www.leafly.com/news/cannabis-101/what-is-the-cole-memo|access-date=January 21, 2018|work=Leafly|date=September 14, 2017}}</ref>  Aside from these situations, the memo generally allowed for the commercial distribution of cannabis in states where such activity had been legalized.  The Cole memo was only a non-binding set of guidelines for [[United States Attorney|federal prosecutors]], however, and therefore did not carry the force of law.

Although the Cole memo was adhered to by federal prosecutors, members of Congress sought the assurances that a legally binding act of legislation could provide.  The McClintock–Polis amendment was hence introduced in the U.S. House (as an attachment to the [[Commerce, Justice, Science, and Related Agencies Appropriations Act, 2016|Commerce, Justice, and Science appropriations bill for fiscal year 2016]]) to prohibit the Justice Department from interfering with the implementation of state cannabis laws regarding both recreational and medical use.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Baca |first1=Ricardo |title=House passes bill to prevent DOJ from interfering in states' medical pot laws |url=https://www.thecannabist.co/2015/06/03/house-medical-pot-marijuana/35704/ |access-date=October 27, 2018 |work=The Cannabist |date=June 3, 2015 |archive-date=September 9, 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240909130631/https://www.thecannabist.co/2015/06/03/house-medical-pot-marijuana/35704/ }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=House Members Reauthorize Measure Protecting State Sanctioned Medical Marijuana Programs |url=http://norml.org/news/2015/06/04/house-members-reauthorize-measure-protecting-state-sanctioned-medical-marijuana-programs |access-date=October 27, 2018 |work=NORML |date=June 4, 2015}}</ref>  The McClintock–Polis amendment was narrowly defeated on June 3, 2015, by a vote of 206 to 222.<ref>{{cite web |title=All Information (Except Text) for H.Amdt.334 to H.R.2578 |url=https://www.congress.gov/amendment/114th-congress/house-amendment/334/all-info |website=congress.gov |date=June 3, 2015 |access-date=September 29, 2019}}</ref>

The Cole memo remained in effect until January 2018 when it was rescinded by [[United States Attorney General|Attorney General]] [[Jeff Sessions]].<ref name="rescission">{{cite news|last1=Gerstein|last2=Lima|first2=Cristiano|first1=Josh|title=Sessions announces end to policy that allowed legal pot to flourish|url=https://www.politico.com/story/2018/01/04/jeff-sessions-marijuana-policy-us-attorney-enforcement-324020|access-date=January 21, 2018|work=Politico|date=January 4, 2018}}</ref>  The intended impact of the rescission was not immediately made clear, however, in regards to what kind of crackdown (if any) on the states would be forthcoming.<ref name="rescission" />  In response to the memo's rescission, the [[STATES Act]] was introduced in Congress after consultation with President [[Donald Trump]],<ref>{{cite news |last1=Angell |first1=Tom |title=Trump To Support Major Marijuana Legislation |url=https://www.marijuanamoment.net/trump-to-support-major-marijuana-legislation/ |access-date=October 27, 2018 |work=Marijuana Moment |date=April 13, 2018}}</ref> to enshrine into law protections that the Cole memo previously provided.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Angell |first1=Tom |title=Senators File Marijuana Bill Following Trump Pledge To Respect State Legalization |url=https://www.marijuanamoment.net/watch-bipartisan-senators-discuss-marijuana-legislation/ |access-date=October 27, 2018 |work=Marijuana Moment |date=June 6, 2018}}</ref>  Trump confirmed to reporters his intent to sign the STATES Act should it be approved by Congress.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Ferner |first1=Matt |title=Trump Supports Relaxing Federal Pot Laws, In Blow To Jeff Sessions |url=https://www.huffingtonpost.com/entry/trump-marijuana-legalization_us_5b1abffbe4b0bbb7a0dc7d8a |access-date=October 27, 2018 |work=HuffPost |date=June 8, 2018}}</ref>

On December 20, 2018, the [[2018 United States farm bill]] was signed into law, legalizing the cultivation of [[hemp]] containing less than 0.3% [[Tetrahydrocannabinol|THC]] at the federal level. [[hemp loophole|An unintended consequence]] of the bill was that it also legalized at the federal level the production of [[delta-8-Tetrahydrocannabinol|delta-8-THC]], an isomer of THC with similar psychoactive effects. Use of delta-8-THC subsequently became popular as an alternative to cannabis in states where cannabis is illegal.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.npr.org/2022/01/04/1070338052/a-loophole-in-federal-marijuana-law-has-led-to-the-creation-of-new-thc-product |title=A loophole in federal marijuana law has led to the creation of new THC product |date=January 4, 2022 |author=Rob Mentzer|website=NPR.org }}</ref>

On June 20, 2019, four years after the McClintock–Polis amendment was defeated, a similar amendment protecting state-legal cannabis activities was approved by the House.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Angell |first1=Tom |title=Congress Votes To Block Feds From Enforcing Marijuana Laws In Legal States |url=https://www.forbes.com/sites/tomangell/2019/06/20/congress-votes-to-block-feds-from-enforcing-marijuana-laws-in-legal-states/ |access-date=July 15, 2019 |work=Forbes |date=June 20, 2019}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Sullum |first1=Jacob |title=House Resoundingly Approves Broad Marijuana Federalism |url=https://reason.com/2019/06/24/house-resoundingly-approves-broad-marijuana-federalism/ |access-date=July 15, 2019 |work=Reason |date=June 24, 2019}}</ref>  The amendment, introduced by Rep. [[Earl Blumenauer]] and attached to the CJS appropriations bill for fiscal year 2020, passed by a 267–165 vote.<ref>{{cite web |title=All Information (Except Text) for H.Amdt.398 to H.R.3055 |url=https://www.congress.gov/amendment/116th-congress/house-amendment/398/all-info |website=congress.gov |date=June 20, 2019 |access-date=September 29, 2019}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Jaeger |first1=Kyle |title=Analysis: Breaking Down Congress's Vote To Protect Legal Marijuana States From Federal Enforcement |url=https://www.marijuanamoment.net/analysis-breaking-down-congresss-vote-to-protect-legal-marijuana-states-from-federal-enforcement/ |access-date=July 15, 2019 |work=Marijuana Moment |date=June 24, 2019}}</ref>

On September 25, 2019, the House of Representatives approved the [[SAFE Banking Act|Secure and Fair Enforcement (SAFE) Banking Act]] by a 321–103 vote.<ref>{{cite press release |date=September 25, 2019 |title=SAFE Banking Act Passes U.S. House of Representatives with Overwhelming, Bipartisan Support |url=https://perlmutter.house.gov/news/documentsingle.aspx?DocumentID=4657 |location=Washington, D.C. |publisher=house.gov}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Jaeger |first1=Kyle |title=House Approves Marijuana Banking Bill In Historic Vote |url=https://www.marijuanamoment.net/house-approves-marijuana-banking-bill/ |access-date=December 12, 2019 |work=Marijuana Moment |date=September 25, 2019}}</ref>  The bill, which seeks to improve access to banks for cannabis businesses, is the first standalone cannabis reform bill approved by either chamber of Congress.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Jaeger |first1=Kyle |title=Breaking Down Congress's Historic Marijuana Banking Vote |url=https://www.marijuanamoment.net/breaking-down-congresss-historic-marijuana-banking-vote/ |access-date=December 12, 2019 |work=Marijuana Moment |date=September 30, 2019}}</ref>

On November 20, 2019, the [[Marijuana Opportunity Reinvestment and Expungement Act|Marijuana Opportunity Reinvestment and Expungement (MORE) Act]] passed the [[United States House Committee on the Judiciary|House Judiciary Committee]] by a 24–10 vote.<ref>{{cite press release |date=November 20, 2019 |title=House Judiciary Passes MORE Act to Decriminalize Marijuana at Federal Level |url=https://judiciary.house.gov/news/press-releases/house-judiciary-passes-more-act-decriminalize-marijuana-federal-level |location=Washington, D.C. |publisher=house.gov}}</ref>  It was the first time a federal bill to legalize cannabis had ever passed a congressional committee.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Jaeger |first1=Kyle |title=Marijuana Legalization Bill Approved By Congressional Committee In Historic Vote |url=https://www.marijuanamoment.net/watch-live-congress-holds-historic-vote-on-bill-to-federally-legalize-marijuana/ |access-date=December 12, 2019 |work=Marijuana Moment |date=November 20, 2019}}</ref> The MORE Act passed the full House of Representatives on December 4, 2020, by a vote of 228–164.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Wu |first1=Nicholas |title=House of Representatives passes marijuana legalization bill |url=https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/politics/2020/12/04/legal-weed-house-vote-marijuana-legalization-bill/3791933001/ |access-date=December 10, 2020 |work=USA Today |date=December 4, 2020}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Jaeger |first1=Kyle |title=House Approves Federal Marijuana Legalization Bill In Historic Vote |url=https://www.marijuanamoment.net/house-approves-federal-marijuana-legalization-bill-in-historic-vote/ |access-date=December 10, 2020 |work=Marijuana Moment |date=December 4, 2020}}</ref> It passed the House for a second time on April 1, 2022, by a vote of 220–204.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Fertig |first1=Natalie |title=House passes marijuana legalization bill (again), but with no clear path forward |url=https://www.politico.com/news/2022/04/01/house-passes-marijuana-legalization-bill-again-but-with-no-clear-path-forward-00022303 |access-date=April 2, 2022 |work=Politico |date=April 1, 2022}}</ref>

On October 6, 2022, President [[Joe Biden]] announced he would pardon all prior federal offenses of simple possession and urge governors to do the same, and directed the Secretary of Health and Human Services and the Attorney General to initiate the administrative process to review expeditiously how cannabis is scheduled under federal law.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Biden |first1=Joseph |title=Statement from President Biden on Marijuana Reform |url=https://bidenwhitehouse.archives.gov/briefing-room/statements-releases/2022/10/06/statement-from-president-biden-on-marijuana-reform/ |website=The White House |date=6 October 2022}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Black |first=Lester |date=2022-10-14 |title=Could Pot Be Decriminalized Before Biden Leaves Office? |url=https://fivethirtyeight.com/features/could-pot-be-decriminalized-before-biden-leaves-office/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221014150239/https://fivethirtyeight.com/features/could-pot-be-decriminalized-before-biden-leaves-office/ |url-status=dead |archive-date=October 14, 2022 |access-date=2022-10-26 |website=FiveThirtyEight |language=en-US}}</ref>

In December 2025, Trump signed an [[executive order]] directing Attorney General [[Pam Bondi]] to expedite the process of changing federal restrictions on cannabis and reclassifying it from a [[Controlled Substances Act|Schedule]] 1 to a Schedule 3 drug.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Owermohle |first=Kit Maher, Steve Contorno, Sarah |date=2025-12-18 |title=Trump signs order expediting marijuana reclassification after lobbying from cannabis industry {{!}} CNN Politics |url=https://www.cnn.com/2025/12/18/politics/trump-marijuana-reclassification-executive-order |access-date=2025-12-19 |website=CNN |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Gomez |first=Luke Fountain, Brandon |date=2025-12-18 |title=Trump signs executive order reclassifying cannabis, opening door to broader weed access |url=https://www.cnbc.com/2025/12/18/trump-pot-reclassification-cannabis-stocks-medicare-cbd.html |access-date=2025-12-19 |website=CNBC |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last1=Brensel |first1=Lauren |last2=Friedman |first2=Amanda |date=2025-12-18 |title=Trump signs executive order to ease marijuana restrictions |url=https://www.politico.com/news/2025/12/18/trump-signs-executive-order-to-ease-marijuana-restrictions-00698021 |access-date=2025-12-19 |website=POLITICO |language=en}}</ref>

== Arguments in support of reform ==
{{More citations needed section|date=January 2016}}

In 1972, President [[Richard Nixon]] commissioned the [[National Commission on Marijuana and Drug Abuse]] to produce an in-depth report on cannabis. The report, "Marijuana: A Signal of Misunderstanding", found cannabis prohibition constitutionally suspect and stated regardless of whether the courts would overturn the prohibition of cannabis possession, the executive and legislative branches have a duty to obey the Constitution.<ref name=nixonreport>{{cite web|title=Marihuana: A Signal of Misunderstanding|publisher=[[National Commission on Marihuana and Drug Abuse]]|date=March 1972|url=http://www.druglibrary.org/schaffer/Library/studies/nc/ncmenu.htm|access-date=2007-04-20}}</ref>
"It's a matter of individual freedom of choice", said ACLU President [[Nadine Strossen]] in an interview. "Does that mean they should do it? Not necessarily, not any more than somebody should smoke or drink or eat [[McDonald's]] hamburgers."<ref>Shankbone, David. "[[n:ACLU President Strossen on religion, drugs, guns and impeaching George Bush#Gun rights|Interview with Nadine Strossen]]" ''[[Wikinews]]'' (2007-10-30). Retrieved 2007-11-14.</ref>

U.S. attitudes toward legalization and decriminalization started dramatically liberalizing in the 1990s, and a 2018 study in [[Social Science Research]] found that the main drivers of these changes in attitudes were a decline in the perception of the riskiness of marijuana, changes in media framing of marijuana, a decline in overall punitiveness, and a decrease in religious affiliation.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Felson |first1=Jacob |last2=Adamczyk |first2=Amy |last3=Thomas |first3=Christopher |title=How and why have attitudes about cannabis legalization changed so much? |journal=Social Science Research |date=February 2019 |volume=78 |pages=12–27 |doi=10.1016/j.ssresearch.2018.12.011 |pmid=30670211 |s2cid=58949541 }}</ref> Additionally, a Gallup poll conducted in 2022 found that 53% of Americans believe that cannabis has a positive effect on people and 45% say that it has a negative effect.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://news.gallup.com/poll/396893/americans-not-convinced-marijuana-benefits-society.aspx|title=Americans Not Convinced Marijuana Benefits Society|date=August 16, 2022}}</ref> Meanwhile, 71% of Americans believe that alcohol has a negative effect on people.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://news.gallup.com/poll/395867/say-alcohol-adversely-affects-drinkers-society.aspx|title=Most in U.S. Say Alcohol Adversely Affects Drinkers, Society|date=August 5, 2022}}</ref>

=== Potential medical benefits of marijuana ===
Marijuana (cannabis) is an herb drug, which contains a very active component delta-9-tetrahydrocannabinol (THC).<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Bostwick |first1=J. Michael |title=Blurred Boundaries: The Therapeutics and Politics of Medical Marijuana |journal=Mayo Clinic Proceedings |date=February 2012 |volume=87 |issue=2 |pages=172–186 |doi=10.1016/j.mayocp.2011.10.003 |pmid=22305029 |pmc=3538401 }}</ref> For thousands of years, it was used for medical purposes in many different parts of the world.<ref>{{Cite book|title=Medical Marijuana and Marijuana Use|last=Johnson|first=Albert T.|publisher=Nova Science Publishers|year=2009|isbn=978-1-60692-899-8|location=New York|pages=27}}</ref> Preliminary evidence has indicated that THC might have several beneficial medical uses.<ref name=Borgelt2013>{{cite journal | vauthors = Borgelt LM, Franson KL, Nussbaum AM, Wang GS | title = The pharmacologic and clinical effects of medical cannabis | journal = Pharmacotherapy | volume = 33 | issue = 2 | pages = 195–209 | date = February 2013 | pmid = 23386598 | doi = 10.1002/phar.1187 | s2cid = 8503107 | url = https://wsma.org/doc_library/LegalResourceCenter/MedicalCannabis/Med%20Mar%20-%20Pharmacologic%20and%20Clinical%20Effects.pdf | access-date = 11 November 2017 | archive-date = 28 August 2021 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20210828161528/https://wsma.org/doc_library/LegalResourceCenter/MedicalCannabis/Med%20Mar%20-%20Pharmacologic%20and%20Clinical%20Effects.pdf | url-status = live }}</ref><ref>{{cite journal | vauthors = Burns TL, Ineck JR | title = Cannabinoid analgesia as a potential new therapeutic option in the treatment of chronic pain | journal = The Annals of Pharmacotherapy | volume = 40 | issue = 2 | pages = 251–60 | date = February 2006 | pmid = 16449552 | doi = 10.1345/aph.1G217 | s2cid = 6858360 | type = Review }}</ref> A number of patients who have HIV, multiple sclerosis (MS), neuropathic chronic pain, and cancer were under medical marijuana treatment. The treatments could either be smoke on cannabis or oral preparations, which were synthetic THC and synthetic equivalent.<ref name=pmid26126326>{{cite journal |last1=Maule |first1=W. J. |title=Medical uses of marijuana (''Cannabis sativa''): fact or fallacy? |journal=British Journal of Biomedical Science |date=4 January 2016 |volume=72 |issue=2 |pages=85–91 |doi=10.1080/09674845.2015.11666802 |pmid=26126326 }}</ref>

According to Medical Uses of Marijuana (Cannabis sativa), patients with HIV reported that the drug reduced mixed neuropathic pain more significantly in comparison to other placebo drugs. They addressed that there was a minimum of 30% pain reduction when they were under smoked medical marijuana treatment. Furthermore, under the same type of treatment, most of the patients with multiple sclerosis showed dramatic improvements in their symptoms.<ref name=pmid26126326/>  After the treatment, their handwriting was much clearer and head tremor pain was less than the samples taken before the treatment. In addition, many patients that associated with chronic pain, multiple sclerosis were also under marijuana oral preparation treatment. Patients treated with dronabinol, a synthetic THC, reported that there was a 50% pain reduction compared to 30% that was experienced when given the placebo. Furthermore, cancer treatment involving chemotherapy also agreed that dronabinol had significant benefits on delaying nausea and vomiting for patients.<ref name=pmid26126326/>

However, medical marijuana treatments are not for everyone and it may cause adverse side effects for others. Overall, the potential long-term side effects of medical marijuana are not yet fully classified. As a result, further studies must carry out to fully understand the benefits as well as adverse psychiatric and medical side effects of the drug.

The medical use of marijuana for pain relief could be safer than traditional opioids used for pain relief as marijuana cannot be overdosed on and is less addictive. Marijuana could also replace the use of common non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs) such as Advil or Aleve that have been known to cause kidney or ulcer problems.<ref name=":0">{{Cite web |last=MD |first=Peter Grinspoon |date=2018-01-15 |title=Medical marijuana |url=https://www.health.harvard.edu/blog/medical-marijuana-2018011513085 |access-date=2022-05-29 |website=Harvard Health |language=en}}</ref> The medical use of marijuana similarly can be used to curb stress-induced cravings, anxiety and lack of impulse control that cause people to relapse in people with drug and alcohol addiction.<ref name=":12">{{Cite web |title=7 Potential Health Benefits of Cannabis |url=https://www.jwu.edu/news/2021/09/7-potential-health-benefits-of-cannabis.html |access-date=2022-05-29 |website=www.jwu.edu |language=en}}</ref> In Colorado and Oregon, the most common use for marijuana is pain, spasticity associated with multiple sclerosis, nausea, posttraumatic stress disorder, cancer, epilepsy, cachexia, glaucoma, HIV/AIDS, and degenerative neurological conditions.<ref>{{Cite book |last1=National Academies of Sciences |first1=Engineering |url=https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK425767/ |title=Therapeutic Effects of Cannabis and Cannabinoids |last2=Division |first2=Health and Medicine |last3=Practice |first3=Board on Population Health and Public Health |last4=Agenda |first4=Committee on the Health Effects of Marijuana: An Evidence Review and Research |date=2017-01-12 |publisher=National Academies Press (US) |language=en}}</ref>

=== Economic arguments ===
Many proponents of cannabis decriminalization have argued that decriminalizing cannabis would largely reduce costs of maintaining the criminal justice and law enforcement systems,<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Caulkins |first1=Jonathan P. |year=2010 |title=Cost of Marijuana Prohibition on the California Criminal Justice System |website=Rand |url=https://www.rand.org/pubs/working_papers/WR763.html}}</ref> while legalizing cannabis to allow the cultivation and sale would generate a substantial amount of income from taxing cannabis sales.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.washingtonpost.com|title=Study: Legal marijuana could generate more than 132 billion in federal tax revenue and 1 million jobs.|last=Zezima|first=Katie|date=January 10, 2018|newspaper=The Washington Post}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news|url=https://itep.org/taxing-cannabis/#performance|title=Taxing Cannabis|last1=Davis|first1=Carl|date=January 23, 2019|work=Institute on Taxation and Economic Policy|last2=Hill|first2=Misha|last3=Phillips|first3=Richard}}</ref> In Colorado, in June 2020, monthly marijuana sales reached $199 million.<ref>[https://www.sapiens.org/column/curiosities/archaeology-marijuana/ An Archaeology of Marijuana How did cannabis, a plant humans have been using for more than 10,000 years, become so vilified in the U.S.?], ''Sapiens magazine'', Stephen E. Nash, October 16, 2020. Retrieved October 16, 2020.</ref> In 2020, state and local governments earned over $3 billion in tax revenue from cannabis taxes.<ref name="Hollenbeck" /> Although some cannabis proponents caution that overtaxation and overregulation of cannabis can result in political corruption, bribery, and an even bigger black market than before legalization.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.latimes.com/california/story/2022-09-08/a-series-on-the-fallout-of-legal-weed-in-california|title=Legal Weed, Broken Promises: A Times series on the fallout of legal pot in California|website=[[Los Angeles Times]] |date=September 8, 2022}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://reason.com/2022/09/22/l-a-times-investigates-californias-marijuana-legalization-disaster/ 
|title=L.A. Times Investigates California's Marijuana Legalization Disaster|date=September 22, 2022}}</ref>

In 2005, more than 530 distinguished economists called for the legalization of cannabis in an open letter to [[George W. Bush|President Bush]], [[United States Congress|Congress]], [[Governors of the United States|Governors]], and state legislatures.<ref name=letter>{{cite web |url=http://www.prohibitioncosts.org/endorsers.html |title=Budgetary Implications of Marijuana Prohibition in the United States |access-date=2008-02-20 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20071017021537/http://prohibitioncosts.org/endorsers.html |archive-date=2007-10-17 }}</ref> The endorsers included conservative economist [[Milton Friedman]] and two other [[Nobel Prize]]-winners, Dr. George Akerlof and Dr. Vernon Smith.<ref name=letter/>

The letter stated, among other things, "We, the undersigned, call your attention to the attached report [which]... shows that marijuana legalization — replacing prohibition with a system of taxation and regulation — would save $7.7 billion per year in state and federal expenditures on prohibition enforcement and produce tax revenues of at least $2.4 billion annually if marijuana were taxed like most consumer goods. If, however, marijuana were taxed similarly to alcohol or tobacco, it might generate as much as $6.2 billion annually...."

We, therefore, urge the country to commence an open and honest debate about marijuana prohibition. We believe such a debate will favor a regime in which marijuana is legal but taxed and regulated like other goods. At a minimum, this debate will force advocates of current policy to show that prohibition has benefits sufficient to justify the cost to taxpayers, foregone tax revenues, and numerous ancillary consequences that result from marijuana prohibition."

The report also projected the tax revenues from decriminalization, by state.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://money.cnn.com/pf/features/lists/taxes_marijuana/table.html|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20050610025252/http://money.cnn.com/pf/features/lists/taxes_marijuana/table.html|url-status=dead|archive-date=June 10, 2005|title=CNN Money: State Marijuana Tax Revenues}}</ref>

Other arguments point out that the funds saved from cannabis decriminalization could be used to enforce laws for other, more serious and violent crimes.<ref name="Hollenbeck" /><ref name=nm>{{cite web|url=http://www.drugpolicy.org/library/gov_johnson2.cfm|title=Report and Recommendations: Governor's Drug Policy Advisory Group|publisher=[[Drug Policy Alliance]]|date=January 2001|access-date=2007-03-20|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070606042550/http://www.drugpolicy.org/library/gov_johnson2.cfm|archive-date=2007-06-06}}</ref>

In 1988, Michael Aldrich and Tod Mikuriya published "Savings in California Marijuana Law Enforcement Costs Attributable to the Moscone Act of 1976" in the ''[[Journal of Psychoactive Drugs]]''. The study estimated [[California]] saved almost one billion dollars in a twelve-year period between 1976 and 1988, as a result of the Moscone Act of 1976 that decriminalized cannabis.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.marijuanalibrary.org/JPD_Savings_1988.txt|title=Savings in California Marijuana Law Enforcement Costs Attributable to the Moscone Act of 1976 – A Summary |publisher= marijuanalibrary.org |access-date= 2007-03-20}}</ref>

In 2003, the [[Office of National Drug Control Policy]] (ONDCP) published "Economic Costs of Drug Abuse", which stated without separately analyzing cannabis related costs, the United States was spending $12.1&nbsp;billion on law enforcement and court costs, and $16.9&nbsp;billion in corrections costs, totaling $29&nbsp;billion.<ref name="norml">{{cite web|url= http://www.norml.org/pdf_files/NORML_Rethinking_Decriminalizing_Marijuana.pdf|author=James Austin|publisher= The JFA Institute (published on [[National Organization for the Reform of Marijuana Laws (United States)|NORML]])|title=Rethinking the Consequences of Decriminalizing Marijuana|date=2005-11-02|access-date=2006-12-24}}</ref>

In 2004, Scott Bates of the Boreal Economic Analysis & Research center prepared a study for Alaskans for Rights & Revenues entitled "The Economic Implications of Marijuana Legalization in Alaska." The study estimated the [[Alaska]]n government was spending $25–30&nbsp;million per year enforcing cannabis prohibition laws. The study found if the purchase of cannabis were to be taxed as a legal commodity, tax revenues would increase by about $10–20&nbsp;million per year, making $35–50&nbsp;million per year in funds available.<ref name=norml/><ref name="prohibcosts">{{cite web|url=http://www.prohibitioncosts.org/FinalBatesreport.pdf|title=The Economic Implications of Marijuana Legalization in Alaska|publisher=prohibitioncosts.org|access-date=2007-03-21|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20061229223818/http://www.prohibitioncosts.org/FinalBatesreport.pdf|archive-date=2006-12-29}}</ref>

In 2006, a study by [[Jon Gettman]] entitled "Marijuana Production in the United States" was published in the ''Bulletin of Cannabis Reform''. The report states cannabis is the top cash crop in 12 states, is one of the top three cash crops in 30 states, and is one of the top five cash crops in 39 states. Gettman estimated the value of U.S. cannabis production at $35.8&nbsp;billion, which is more than the combined value of corn and wheat. Furthermore, the report states according to federal estimates, eradication efforts have failed to prevent the spread of cannabis production, as cannabis production has increased tenfold in the past 25 years.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.drugscience.org/Archive/bcr2/MJCropReport_2006.pdf|title=Marijuana Production in the United States|website=drugscience.org|year=2006|access-date=2007-03-20|author=Jon Gettman|archive-date=April 28, 2008|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080428042715/http://www.drugscience.org/Archive/bcr2/MJCropReport_2006.pdf}}</ref>

In 2006, the [[United Nations Office on Drugs and Crime]] released the 2006 World Drug Report, which stated the North American cannabis market is estimated to be worth anywhere from $10&nbsp;billion to $60&nbsp;billion annually.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://baltimorechronicle.com/2006/091506HICKMAN.shtml|title=UNODC Makes the Case for Ending Cannabis Prohibition—Inadvertently|first=John|last=Hickman|newspaper=[[Baltimore Chronicle]]|access-date=2007-06-20}}</ref>  That same study also indicated that the mountainous regions in Appalachia, and the rural areas of the West Coast are ideal for growing cannabis. Allowing farmers there to grow cannabis openly would both provide jobs and reduce the need for expensive federal welfare payments to those areas, which are disproportionately dependent on welfare.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.statemaster.com/graph/eco_wel_cas_tot_rec_percap-caseloads-total-recipients-per-capita|title=Welfare Caseloads Total recipients (per capita) (most recent) by state|access-date=November 16, 2009|archive-date=January 11, 2010|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100111042401/http://www.statemaster.com/graph/eco_wel_cas_tot_rec_percap-caseloads-total-recipients-per-capita}}</ref>

In 2006, a study by the [[University of California, Los Angeles]] found [[California]] has saved $2.50 for every dollar invested into [[California Proposition 36 (2000)|Proposition 36]], which decriminalized cannabis and other drug possession charges by allowing outpatient treatment programs instead of incarceration. In the first year, the proposition was enacted (2001), California reportedly saved $173&nbsp;million, which is likely a result of fewer drug offenders in prison. In the five years after the program was enacted, 8,700 fewer people are in prison for drug offenses.<ref>{{cite news |author=Jason Ziedenberg |author2=Rose Braz |url=http://www.signonsandiego.com/uniontrib/20060417/news_mz1e17zieden.html |title=Saving money and aiding drug users |work=[[The San Diego Union-Tribune]] |date=2006-04-17 |access-date=2007-03-23 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070506150333/http://www.signonsandiego.com/uniontrib/20060417/news_mz1e17zieden.html |archive-date=2007-05-06 }}</ref>

A 2020 study by researchers at [[UCLA]] and [[Yale]] found that cannabis legalization had led to large amounts of new tax revenue and very little black market cannabis sales in states where the market was well-regulated, but large black markets and lack of tax revenue in states like California with poor regulation.<ref name="Hollenbeck">{{cite journal |last1=Hollenbeck |first1=Brett |last2=Uetake |first2=Kosuke |title=Taxation and Market Power in the Legal Marijuana Industry |journal=RAND Journal of Economics |date=2021 |volume=52 |issue=3 |pages=559–595 |doi=10.1111/1756-2171.12384 |ssrn=3237729 |url=https://papers.ssrn.com/sol3/papers.cfm?abstract_id=3237729}}</ref>

Since cannabis is illegal in the United States, this policy has led to penalties for simple use and possession. Despite these penalties, users continue to find themselves in trouble with the law. The Connecticut Law Revision Commission made the following evaluation:
"(1) the costs of arresting and prosecuting marijuana offenders were significantly lower in states that had done away with criminal penalties for possessing small amounts; (2) there was a greater increase in marijuana use in states that continue to treat possession as a crime than in states that treated it as a civil offense; (3) easing the penalties for marijuana did not lead to a substantial increase in the use of either alcohol or hard drugs."<ref>Ruschmann, J.D. (2004). ''Legalizing Marijuana'', Paul Chelsea House Publishers.</ref>

=== Reduction of income earned by organized crime ===
The [[Drug Enforcement Administration]] has reported that cannabis sales and trafficking support violent criminal gangs.<ref>{{cite web|url= http://www.usdoj.gov/ndic/pubs11/12620/marijuana.htm |title=National Drug Threat Assessment 2006|publisher=[[National Drug Intelligence Center]]|date=February 2005|access-date=2007-04-02}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.usdoj.gov/ndic/pubs8/8731/marijuana.htm|title=National Drug Threat Assessment 2004|publisher=[[National Drug Intelligence Center]]|date=April 2004|access-date=2007-04-02}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.usdoj.gov/ndic/pubs/653/marijuan.htm|title=California Northern and Eastern Districts Drug Threat Assessment|publisher=[[National Drug Intelligence Center]]|date=January 2001|access-date=2007-04-03}}</ref> Proponents of fully decriminalizing cannabis to allow the regulated cultivation and sale of cannabis, including [[Law Enforcement Against Prohibition]], argue that fully decriminalizing cannabis would largely decrease financial gains earned by gangs in black market cannabis sales and trafficking.<ref name=msnbc1>{{cite news|url=https://www.nbcnews.com/id/wbna15305579|date=2006-10-17|title=Nevadans to vote on legalizing marijuana|work=[[NBC News]]|access-date=2007-03-27}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://leap.cc/Speakers/speakerbio.php?spkr=./Bios/becker.inc&name=Dean%20Becker|title=Dean Becker|publisher=[[Law Enforcement Against Prohibition]]|access-date=2007-04-03}}{{dead link|date=January 2018 |bot=InternetArchiveBot |fix-attempted=yes }}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.reviewjournal.com/lvrj_home/2002/Nov-06-Wed-2002/news/20007394.html|author=Ed Vogel|title=Legalization Initiative: Marijuana measure opposed|work=[[Las Vegas Review-Journal]]|date=2006-04-11|access-date=2007-04-19}}</ref>

===Displacement of alcohol consumption===
A study in the ''[[Journal of Policy Analysis and Management]]'' by Mark Anderson and Daniel Reese indicated that increased marijuana use in California is a substitute for alcohol. This research showed that participants frequently choose marijuana over other substances. They reported that over 41 percent of the people said that they prefer to use marijuana instead of alcohol. Some of the main reasons for this substitute were 'less withdrawal', 'fewer side-effects' and 'better symptom management'.<ref>{{Cite news|author=Paul Armentano |url=http://www.alternet.org/drugs/marijuana-exit-drug-study-suggests-some-are-taking-it-substitute-prescription-drugs-and |title=Is Marijuana an 'Exit Drug'? Study Suggests Some Are Taking It as a Substitute for Prescription Drugs and Alcohol |journal=AlterNet |date=2012-12-26 |access-date=2014-05-04}}</ref><ref>{{cite news | url=https://www.nytimes.com/2013/10/27/us/few-problems-with-cannabis-for-california.html?_r=0 | title=Few Problems With Cannabis for California | newspaper=[[The New York Times]] | date=October 26, 2013 | access-date=2014-05-05 |author1=Naggourney, Adam |author2=Lyman, Rick }}</ref>

[[Secretary of State of California|California Secretary of State's office]] said that on September 7, 2010, the beer lobby donated $10,000 to Public Safety First, a group which opposed the passage of Proposition 19 to legalize cannabis.<ref name=laweekly>{{cite news |author=J. Patrick Coolican |url=http://www.laweekly.com/informer/2010/09/21/beer-lobby-gives-10000-to-no-on-proposition-19-pot-legalization |title=Beer Lobby Gives $10,000 To 'No' On Proposition 19 Pot Legalization |newspaper=LA Weekly |date=2013-10-03 |access-date=2014-05-04 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140503000347/http://www.laweekly.com/informer/2010/09/21/beer-lobby-gives-10000-to-no-on-proposition-19-pot-legalization |archive-date=2014-05-03 }}</ref>

=== Reduction of subsequent use of other illicit drugs ===
The Marijuana Policy Project argues that:<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.mpp.org/assets/pdfs/download-materials/TandRARGUMENTS092008.pdf |title=Effective Arguments for Advocates of Taxing and Regulating Marijuana |access-date=2008-10-20 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080918173659/http://www.mpp.org/assets/pdfs/download-materials/TandRARGUMENTS092008.pdf |archive-date=2008-09-18 }}</ref>

{{blockquote|Research shows that the actual "gateway" is the illegal drug market. The World Health Organization noted that any gateway effect associated with marijuana use may actually be due to marijuana prohibition because "exposure to other drugs when purchasing cannabis on the black market increases the opportunity to use other illicit drugs." A study comparing experienced cannabis users in Amsterdam, where adults can purchase small amounts of cannabis from regulated businesses, with similarly experienced cannabis users in San Francisco, where non-medical possession and sale of cannabis remains completely illegal, bolstered this hypothesis: The San Francisco cannabis users were twice as likely to use crack cocaine as their Dutch counterparts, more than twice as likely to use amphetamines, and five times as likely to be current users of opiates.}}

=== Health effects of cannabis ===
{{See also|Effects of cannabis}}
[[File:Rational scale to assess the harm of drugs (mean physical harm and mean dependence).svg|thumb|Comparison of the perceived harm for various psychoactive drugs from a poll among medical psychiatrists specialized in addiction treatment (David Nutt et al. 2007)<ref name="Nutt">{{cite journal |last1=Nutt |first1=David |last2=King |first2=Leslie A |last3=Saulsbury |first3=William |last4=Blakemore |first4=Colin |title=Development of a rational scale to assess the harm of drugs of potential misuse |journal=The Lancet |date=March 2007 |volume=369 |issue=9566 |pages=1047–1053 |doi=10.1016/S0140-6736(07)60464-4 |pmid=17382831 |bibcode=2007Lanc..369.1047N |s2cid=5903121 }}</ref>]]

Cannabis has been subject to many studies over the past century. Studies acknowledge that cannabis can in rare cases cause adverse reactions, but is generally safer than any commonly consumed drug such as [[alcohol (drug)|alcohol]], [[tobacco]] and [[pharmaceuticals]].<ref>{{Cite book|url=http://www.saferchoice.org/content/view/24/53/ |title=Safer – Marijuana vs. Alcohol |publisher=Saferchoice.org |date=2006-05-25 |access-date=2013-07-06|isbn=978-1-60358-510-1 }}</ref><ref>{{cite journal |last1=Pletcher |first1=Mark J. |last2=Vittinghoff |first2=Eric |last3=Kalhan |first3=Ravi |last4=Richman |first4=Joshua |author5=[[Monika Safford]]|last6=Sidney |first6=Stephen |last7=Lin |first7=Feng |last8=Kertesz |first8=Stefan |title=Association Between Marijuana Exposure and Pulmonary Function Over 20 Years |journal=JAMA |date=11 January 2012 |volume=307 |issue=2 |pages=173–81 |doi=10.1001/jama.2011.1961 |pmid=22235088 |pmc=3840897}}</ref><ref>{{cite press release |date=January 11, 2012 |title=Marijuana smoke not as damaging to lungs as cigarette smoke, study suggests |website=ScienceDaily |url=https://www.sciencedaily.com/releases/2012/01/120110163444.htm}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.vimm.ca/medical/marijuana-versus-addictive-pharmaceuticals/ |title=Marijuana versus Addictive Pharmaceuticals |publisher=Vimm.ca |access-date=2013-07-06}}</ref> In fact, in an article published in ''The Lancet'' journal about the adverse health effects of non-medical cannabis use, Professors Hall and Degenhardt clearly stated that "the public health burden of cannabis use is probably modest compared with that of alcohol, tobacco, and other illicit drugs."<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Hall |first1=Wayne |last2=Degenhardt |first2=Louisa |title=Adverse health effects of non-medical cannabis use |journal=The Lancet |date=October 2009 |volume=374 |issue=9698 |pages=1383–1391 |doi=10.1016/S0140-6736(09)61037-0 |pmid=19837255 |s2cid=31616272 }}</ref>  Psychopharmacologist and former UK government drugs advisor [[David Nutt]] argues, though he is against full declassification, that the harm caused by cannabis is far less than that caused by alcohol or tobacco, which, if they were invented today "would be illegal".<ref name="Nutt"/><ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.theguardian.com/society/2010/nov/01/alcohol-more-harmful-than-heroin-crack|title=Alcohol 'more harmful than heroin or crack'|last1=Boseley|first1=Sarah|date=2010-11-01|work=The Guardian|access-date=2019-05-26 |language=en-GB|issn=0261-3077}}</ref> A 1933 report by the U.S. Army, titled ''[[Mariajuana Smoking in Panama]]'', found that "There is no evidence that mariahuana as grown here is a 'habit-forming' drug in the sense in which the term is applied to alcohol, opium, cocaine, etc., or that it has any appreciably deleterious influence on the individuals using it."<ref>{{citation |title=MARIAJUANA SMOKING IN PANAMA |url=https://www.druglibrary.org/schaffer/library/studies/panama/panama1.htm |date=1933 |journal=The Military Surgeon |volume=73 |access-date=July 25, 2022}}</ref>

=== Reduction in prison overcrowding and strain on the criminal justice system ===
Supporters of decriminalization and [[decarceration in the United States]] argue that if cannabis were to be legalized it would reduce the number of non-violent offenders in prison making room for the incarceration of more violent offenders as well as easing the current strain that the large number of cannabis possession cases have on the criminal justice system. They also propose that it would also save taxpayers the cost of incarceration for these non-violent offenders.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.umsl.edu/~keelr/180/2003_pot_arrests.htm |title=Marijuana Arrests at All-Time High, Far Exceed Violent Crime Arrests |publisher=Umsl.edu |date=2004-10-29 |access-date=2010-09-15}}</ref>

In the 1970s, there were just under 200,000 criminals serving time in state and federal prisons and upwards of 750,000 in local jails for marijuana-related crimes. Today there are over 1.5&nbsp;million Americans serving time in an institution. If marijuana was decriminalized, these numbers were further be reduced again to below 700,000 inmates and save the taxpayers billions of dollars per year.<ref>{{Cite web|url = http://norml.org/news/2007/11/21/decriminalizing-pot-will-reduce-prison-population-have-no-adverse-impact-on-public-safety-study-says|title = Unlocking America: Why and How to Reduce America's Prison Population|date = 21 November 2007|access-date = 2014-10-24|website = Decriminalizing Pot Will Reduce Prison Population, Have No Adverse Impact On Public Safety, Study Says|publisher = NORML}}</ref>

The United States spends an estimated $68&nbsp;billion per year on prisoners with a third of that number have been incarcerated for non-violent drug crimes including a sixth of those numbers as marijuana drug-related offenses. A reduction in the prison population due to decriminalizing marijuana could save an average of $11.3&nbsp;billion per year on courts, police, prison guards, and other related expenses.<ref>{{Cite web|url = http://sundial.csun.edu/2009/11/legalizing-marijuana-can-reduce-crime-increase-revenue-for-state/|title = Daily Sundial|date = 9 November 2009|access-date = 2014-10-24|website = Legalizing marijuana can reduce crime, increase revenue for state|publisher = The Sundial California State University Northridge|last = Wolff|first = Madeline}}</ref>

=== Success of progressive drug policies adopted in other countries ===
Studies on decriminalization of marijuana in [[Drug policy of Portugal|Portugal]] have indicated it to be a "huge success".<ref name="healthland.time.com">{{cite news|last=Szalavitz |first=Maia |url=https://healthland.time.com/2010/11/23/portugals-drug-experience-new-study-confirms-decriminalization-was-a-success/ |title=Portugal's Drug Experience: New Study Confirms Decriminalization Was a Success |publisher=[[Time (magazine)|Time]] |date=2010-11-23 |access-date=2013-07-06}}</ref> Drug use rates in Portugal were found to be dramatically lower than the United States with decriminalization enacted.<ref name="healthland.time.com"/>

Teenage use of marijuana in the Netherlands where it is [[Drug policy of the Netherlands|sold legally and openly]] is lower than in the United States.<ref>{{cite news|last=Downs |first=David |url=http://www.eastbayexpress.com/LegalizationNation/archives/2011/09/22/going-dutch-teen-marijuana-use-in-the-us-vs-netherlands-the-full-interview-with-cal-professor-robert-maccoun |title=Going Dutch: Teen Marijuana Use in the US vs. Netherlands — the Full Interview with Cal Professor Robert MacCoun &#124; Legalization Nation |newspaper=East Bay Express |date=2011-09-22 |access-date=2013-07-06}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.drugwarfacts.org/cms/netherlands_v_us |title=The Netherlands Compared With The United States |publisher=Drug War Facts |access-date=2013-07-06}}</ref>

[[Uruguay]] became the first country in the world to completely legalize [[Cannabis in Uruguay|cannabis]] in 2013.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Maier |first1=Shana L. |last2=Mannes |first2=Suzanne |last3=Koppenhofer |first3=Emily L. |title=The Implications of Marijuana Decriminalization and Legalization on Crime in the United States |journal=Contemporary Drug Problems |date=8 May 2017 |volume=44 |issue=2 |pages=125–146 |doi=10.1177/0091450917708790 |s2cid=157234573 }}</ref>

=== Individual freedom ===
Some people are in favor of decriminalization and legalization of marijuana simply for the moral stance that individuals' freedom for [[property rights]] should be respected. This view is generally held in [[libertarianism|libertarian]] politics.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.lp.org/blogs/nigel-lyons/regulate-marijuana-like-wine|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120120115652/http://www.lp.org/blogs/nigel-lyons/regulate-marijuana-like-wine|archive-date=2012-01-20|title=Regulate Marijuana like Wine|work=Libertarian Party}}</ref> This view is that regardless of any health effects of someone's lifestyle choice if they are not directly harming anyone else or their property then they should be free to do what they want. Many people who support drug freedom policies may personally be strongly against drug use themselves but still want to protect the freedom of others to do so.<ref>{{Cite news|url=http://www.thenation.com/blog/171252/congressmen-pressure-obama-new-marijuana-laws|title=Barney Frank, Ron Paul Pressure Obama on New Marijuana Laws|journal=The Nation|date=14 November 2012}}</ref>

===Investors===

To effectively campaign to legalize recreational cannabis use, millions of dollars have been spent to lobby for this reform. [[George Soros]] is a billionaire hedge fund manager that has spent over $25&nbsp;million on marijuana reform efforts.<ref name=ForbesSoros>{{cite magazine|url=https://www.forbes.com/sites/chloesorvino/2014/10/02/an-inside-look-at-the-biggest-drug-reformer-in-the-country-george-soros/|title=An Inside Look At The Biggest Drug Reformer In The Country: George Soros|magazine=Forbes|date=2014-10-02|author=Chloe Sorvino|access-date=2015-02-25}}</ref> In 2010 Soros wrote an op-ed in the ''[[The Wall Street Journal|Wall Street Journal]]'' citing the fact that African Americans are no more likely than other Americans to use marijuana but are far more likely to be arrested for possession.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.theguardian.com/politics/2010/oct/26/soros-marijuana-donation |title=George Soros gives $1m to California's pro-cannabis campaign |newspaper=The Guardian |author=Suzanne Goldenberg |date=2010-10-26 |access-date=2014-05-04}}</ref>

Soros' efforts to reform marijuana laws were predated by fellow billionaire, the late Peter Lewis. Lewis was the former chairman of Progressive Insurance and died on November 23, 2013.<ref name=forbes>{{cite magazine|url=https://www.forbes.com/sites/luisakroll/2013/11/24/billionaire-peter-lewis-who-fought-to-legalize-marjuana-has-died/ |title=Billionaire Peter Lewis, Advocate Of Marijuana Legalization, Dies At 80 |magazine=Forbes |author=Luisa Kroll |date=2013-11-24 |access-date=2014-05-04}}</ref> Lewis is considered to be the most high-profile billionaire backer of drug reform and the [[National Organization for the Reform of Marijuana Laws]] (NORML) estimated that Lewis had spent well over $40&nbsp;million funding the cause since the 1980s.<ref name=forbes/> During the November 2012 election, he spent almost $3&nbsp;million helping secure the passage of marijuana legalization bills in both Washington State and Massachusetts.<ref name=forbes/>
The list of capitalists who have joined Soros and Lewis in the cause of Marijuana reform include John Sperling, who is the founder of the University of Phoenix, and George Zimmer who is the founder and former CEO of Men's Wearhouse. Sperling donated $70,000 to support marijuana law reform in Oregon,<ref>{{cite web|url=http://truth-out.org/news/item/9260-marijuana-advocates-spend-big-and-defeat-drug-war-proponent-in-oregon |title=Marijuana Advocates Spend Big and Defeat Drug War Proponent in Oregon |publisher=Truthout |date=2012-05-20 |access-date=2014-05-04 |author=Zaid Jilani}}</ref>  and Zimmer contributed $20,000 to advocate for marijuana decriminalization in California.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.cnbc.com/2010/04/20/the-marijuana-lobby-all-grown-up.html |title=The Marijuana Lobby: All Grown Up |author=Rob Reuteman |publisher=CNBC |date=2010-04-20 |access-date=2014-05-04}}</ref>

These capitalists have helped pave the way for a new type of business with special interests in the cannabis industry. The ArcView Group was founded in 2010 by Silicon Valley entrepreneurs and marijuana advocates Troy Dayton and Steve DeAngelo.<ref name=wsj>{{cite press release|url=https://www.wsj.com/article/PR-CO-20130502-904140.html |title=ArcView Angel Investors Commit Over $1 Million to Marijuana Industry Start-ups at Seattle Event |publisher=Online.wsj.com |date=2013-05-02 |access-date=2014-05-04}}</ref> Their company teams up angel investors with companies that produce cannabis products and it's been one of the major sources of startup revenue for cannabis-related companies.<ref name=wsj/> This company has contributed hundreds of thousands of dollars to educational reform groups like the Students for [[Students for Sensible Drug Policy|Sensible Drug Policy]] and a pro-legalization PAC run by the Marijuana Policy Project.<ref name=wsj/>

===The ACLU and NAACP===
The ACLU takes a firm position that decriminalizing cannabis will keep tens of thousands of people from entering into the criminal justice system<ref name="marijuana-law-reform">{{cite web|url=https://www.aclu.org/criminal-law-reform/marijuana-law-reform |title=Marijuana Law Reform |publisher=American Civil Liberties Union |access-date=2014-05-04}}</ref> as police efforts result in both unnecessary arrests and the enforcement of marijuana laws wastes billions of tax payers' dollars.<ref name=billions>{{cite web|url=https://www.aclu.org/billions-dollars-wasted-racially-biased-arrests |title=Billions of Dollars Wasted on Racially Biased Arrests |publisher=American Civil Liberties Union |access-date=2014-05-04}}</ref> They affirm that removing criminal penalties for marijuana offenses will therefore reduce the U.S. prison population and more effectively protect the public and promote public health.<ref name="marijuana-law-reform"/> One of the reasons that the ACLU has been such a strong supporter of drug decriminalization is that according to their research drug-related arrests have largely driven America's incarceration rate to unacceptable levels. Drug offenders account for over 500,000 of the more than 2&nbsp;million people in America's prisons and jails, and drug offenses combined with failed drug tests account for a significant number of those returning to prison for parole and probation violations.<ref name="marijuana-law-reform"/> Between 2001 and 2010, there were over 7&nbsp;million pot arrests in the U.S., and of these arrests 88% were for simply having marijuana.<ref name="aclu-marijuana-arrests-numbers">{{cite web|title=Marijuana Arrests By The Numbers|url=https://www.aclu.org/gallery/marijuana-arrests-numbers|website=American Civil Liberties Union|access-date=2018-01-29|archive-date=December 30, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201230004233/https://www.aclu.org/gallery/marijuana-arrests-numbers}}</ref> These marijuana-related arrests now account for over half of all drug-related arrests in the United States.<ref name="marijuana-law-reform"/> These arrests tend to be racially imbalanced as a black person is 3.73 times more likely to be arrested than a white person for marijuana-related charges,<ref name="marijuana-law-reform"/> despite research that suggests fairly equal usage rates between the two races.<ref name="aclu-marijuana-arrests-numbers"/> The ACLU is further troubled by the amount of money that is spent annually to enforce marijuana laws as they claim that over 3&nbsp;billion dollars are spent every year by states to enforce marijuana regulation,<ref name="aclu-marijuana-arrests-numbers"/> while the drug's availability has not declined.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.aclu.org/criminal-law-reform/war-marijuana-black-and-white-report |title=The War on Marijuana in Black and White: Report |publisher=American Civil Liberties Union |date=2013-06-03 |access-date=2014-05-04}}</ref> The ACLU claims that over 50% of Americans support marijuana legalization<ref name="aclu-marijuana-arrests-numbers"/> and they are advocating for the legalization of Cannabis through the Criminal Law Reform Project.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.aclu.org/issues/criminal-law-reform |title=Criminal Law Reform |publisher=American Civil Liberties Union |access-date=2014-05-04}}</ref> They believe that the resources that are spent on enforcing marijuana law could be better invested in our communities through education and job training.<ref name=billions/>

The NAACP has taken a similar stance and has cited the same data used by the ACLU.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.politifact.com/rhode-island/statements/2014/mar/16/naacp/new-england-naacp-says-african-americans-us-are-ar/ |title=New England NAACP says African-Americans in U.S. are arrested on marijuana charges at more than three times the rate of whites |publisher=Politifact |access-date=2014-05-04}}</ref> The NAACP has been strong supporters of the [[Legal history of cannabis in the United States|Respect State Marijuana Laws Act]] – H.R. 1523 and has reached out to members of congress to get this act passed.<ref>{{cite news|url= http://www.huffingtonpost.com/2013/11/09/marijuana-naacp_n_4246693.html |title=Bipartisan Marijuana Bill Appears To Have One More Big Supporter In Its Corner: The NAACP |work=The Huffington Post |date=2013-11-09 |access-date=2014-05-04 |author=Chris Gentilviso}}</ref> This act is designed to decrease penalties for low-level marijuana possession and supports prohibiting federal enforcement of marijuana laws in states which have lesser penalties.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.naacp.org/press/entry/naacp-passes-resolution-on-hr.-1523 |title=NAACP Passes Resolution on HR. 1523 |publisher=NAACP |date=2013-11-13 |access-date=2014-05-04 |archive-url=http://webarchive.loc.gov/all/20140206014250/http%3A//www.naacp.org/press/entry/naacp%2Dpasses%2Dresolution%2Don%2Dhr.%2D1523 |archive-date=2014-02-06 }}</ref>

=== Racial bias ===
There are claims of historical evidence showing that a significant reason for the marijuana ban by the US government was political and racist in nature, aimed to suppress black and Mexican minorities.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.businessinsider.com/racist-origins-marijuana-prohibition-legalization-2018-2|title=The racist origins of marijuana prohibition|last=Pagano|first=Alyssa|website=Business Insider|access-date=2019-05-26|date=2018-03-02}}</ref> A quote from a 1934 newspaper reads:

<blockquote>"Marihuana influences Negroes to look at white people in the eye, step on white men's shadows and look at a white woman twice."<ref>{{cite web|last1=Guither|first1=Pete|title=Why is Marijuana Illegal|url=http://www.drugwarrant.com/articles/why-is-marijuana-illegal/|website=drugwarrant.com}}</ref></blockquote>

Former Nixon aide and Watergate co-conspirator [[John Ehrlichman]] said the following to author Dan Baum in an interview regarding the politics of drug prohibition:

<blockquote>"The Nixon campaign in 1968, and the Nixon White House after that, had two enemies: the antiwar left and black people. You understand what I'm saying? We knew we couldn't make it illegal to be either against the war or black, but by getting the public to associate the hippies with marijuana and blacks with heroin, and then criminalizing both heavily, we could disrupt those communities. We could arrest their leaders, raid their homes, break up their meetings, and vilify them night after night on the evening news. Did we know we were lying about the drugs? Of course we did."<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://harpers.org/archive/2016/04/legalize-it-all/|title=[Report] Legalize it All|last=Baum|first=Dan|date=April 2016|work=Harpers Magazine|access-date=May 26, 2019}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.cnn.com/2016/03/23/politics/john-ehrlichman-richard-nixon-drug-war-blacks-hippie/index.html|title=Report: Aide says Nixon's war on drugs targeted blacks, hippies|last=LoBianco|first=Tom|date=March 24, 2016|work=CNN|access-date=May 26, 2019}}</ref></blockquote>

Loo, Hoorens, Hof, and Kahan also talked about this issue in their book 'Cannabis policy, implementation and outcomes'. According to them, statistics show that controlling cannabis use leads in many cases to selective law enforcement, which increases the chances of arresting people from certain ethnicities. For example, while Blacks and Hispanics constitute about 20% of cannabis users in the US, they accounted for 58% of cannabis offenders sentenced under federal law in 1994.<ref>Loo, M. v., Hoorens, S., Hof, C. v., & Kahan, J. P. (2003). Cannabis policy and social consequences. Cannabis policy, implementation, and outcomes (50–63). Santa Monica, CA: Rand.</ref>

In 2013, the [[American Civil Liberties Union|ACLU]] published a report titled "The War on Marijuana in Black and White". The report found that despite marijuana use being roughly equal between blacks and whites, blacks are 3.73 times as likely to be arrested for marijuana possession.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.aclu.org/report/report-war-marijuana-black-and-white?redirect=criminal-law-reform/war-marijuana-black-and-white|title=Report: The War on Marijuana in Black and White|date=June 2013|work=American Civil Liberties Union|access-date=May 26, 2019}}</ref>

Tough marijuana policies have also resulted in the disproportionate mass deportation of over 250,000 legal immigrants in the United States.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.drugpolicy.org/sites/default/files/DPA%20Fact%20Sheet_The%20Drug%20War%20and%20Mass%20Deportation_%28Feb.%202016%29.pdf|title=The Drug War and Mass Deportation|date=February 2016|work=The Drug Policy Alliance|access-date=May 26, 2019}}</ref> In a 93-page report, [[Human Rights Watch]] described the effects of stringent marijuana and other drug policies on US immigrant families.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.hrw.org/report/2015/06/16/a-price-too-high/us-families-torn-apart-by-deportations-for-drug-offenses|title=A Price Too High: US families Torn Apart by Deportations for Drug Offenses|last=Meng|first=Grace|date=June 16, 2015|work=Human Rights Watch|access-date=May 26, 2019}}</ref>

In New York City, Black and Brown people were the most affected when it came to arrests relating to Marijuana accounting for 94% of all persons in 2020.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Hallum |first=Mark |date=March 10, 2021 |title=People of color made up 94% of marijuana arrests by NYPD in 2020, data and Legal Aid says |url=https://www.amny.com/news/people-of-color-made-up-94-of-marijuana-arrests-by-nypd-in-2020-data-and-legal-aid-says/ |access-date=May 29, 2022 |website=amny.com}}</ref> In Texas, overall arrests for marijuana fell for Blacks from 64,826 in 2017 to 63,019 in 2018 and 24,890 in 2020 to 22,496 in 2021. However, looking at primarily arrests for Blacks, it increased from 19,040 in 2017 to 19,760 in 2018 and 7,457 in 2020 to 7,466 in 2021.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2022-04-20 |title=Texas NORML's Marijuana Possession Report 2017-2021 |url=https://www.texasnorml.org/tx-norml-pom-report-2017-2021/ |access-date=2022-05-30 |website=Texas NORML |language=en-US}}</ref> According to the American Civil Liberties Union (ACLU), Black people are 3.64 times more like than white people to be arrested for marijuana related offenses. With more and more states legalizing marijuana, trends related to Black people being arrested have not changed since 2010.<ref>{{Cite web |title=A Tale of Two Countries: Racially Targeted Arrests in the Era of Marijuana Reform |url=https://www.aclu.org/report/tale-two-countries-racially-targeted-arrests-era-marijuana-reform |access-date=2022-05-30 |website=American Civil Liberties Union |language=en}}</ref>

=== Occupational health and safety ===
Since cannabis is still recognized as an illegal substance under federal law, each state has its own rules and regulations concerning cannabis cultivation. As this is still a relatively new industry, there are challenges in formulating safety regulations; much discrepancy exists between state regulations and federal regulations concerning legal agricultural practices.<ref name="dilemma">{{cite journal |last1=Stone |first1=Dave |title=Cannabis, pesticides and conflicting laws: The dilemma for legalized States and implications for public health |journal=Regulatory Toxicology and Pharmacology |date=August 2014 |volume=69 |issue=3 |pages=284–288 |doi=10.1016/j.yrtph.2014.05.015 |pmid=24859075 }}</ref> Since there are no federal regulations on pesticide use in cannabis cultivation, none are registered for use in the United States, and illegal pesticide use is common. Samples purchased by law enforcement in California, have for example detected pesticide residues present on cannabis products for sale to the public.<ref name="dilemma" />  Workers risk exposure to THC, pesticides, and fertilizers through respiratory, dermal, and ocular pathways. One grower was reported to have developed [[pruritus]] and contact [[Hives|uticaria]] from simply handling the plants, after being tolerant to moderate use before. Other allergic reactions, such as asthma, rhinitis, conjunctivitis, and cutaneous symptoms have been reported.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.ehstoday.com/safety/study-cannabis-workers-value-safety-lack-formal-training|title=Study: Cannabis Workers Value Safety but Lack Formal Training|date=2018-03-27|work=EHS Today|access-date=2018-10-30}}</ref> Workers are also at risk of overexposure to UV rays from lamps used, and overexposure to carbon dioxide, carbon monoxide, and nitrogen oxides from devices used to promote cannabis growth. Cuts, nicks, and scrapes are also a risk during the harvest of cannabis buds. A survey completed by the CDPHE has found that while workers valued safety, 46%<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://workersafety.3m.com/growing-worker-safety-concerns-medical-marijuana-industry/|title=Growing Worker Safety Concerns in the Medical Marijuana Industry|date=April 17, 2018|website=3M|access-date=October 30, 2018}}</ref> of them never received any training in safety procedures and protocols. Washington and [https://cdphe.colorado.gov/marijuana/cannabis-occupational-safety-and-health Colorado] have published valuable state guides with state regulations and best practices.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://cdphe.colorado.gov/marijuana/cannabis-occupational-safety-and-health|title=Marijuana occupational safety and health – Department of Public Health and Environment|website=www.colorado.gov|language=en|access-date=2018-10-30|date=2017-02-03}}</ref>

=== Environmental safety ===
'''Pesticide Use''': "The [[United States Environmental Protection Agency|Environmental Protection Agency (EPA)]] regulates pesticide use on agricultural crops, but has not tested any pesticides for use on marijuana because it is still illegal at the federal level. Given what is known about the chemicals commonly used on marijuana plants, that means a potential public health hazard for the millions of people who smoke or consume marijuana, as well as those who work at the grow operations."<ref name=":1">{{Cite news|url=https://www.npr.org/2015/06/17/413429435/growing-concern-over-unregulated-pesticide-use-among-marijuana-growers|title=Concern Grows Over Unregulated Pesticide Use Among Marijuana Growers|work=NPR.org|access-date=2018-10-30|language=en}}</ref>

According to a 2013 study published in the ''Journal of Toxicology'' that assessed quantities of pesticides marijuana smokers were exposed to, it was found that "recoveries of residues were as high as 69.5% depending on the device used and the component investigated, suggesting that the potential of pesticide and chemical residue exposures to cannabis users is substantial and may pose a significant toxicological threat in the absence of adequate regulatory frameworks".<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Sullivan |first1=Nicholas |last2=Elzinga |first2=Sytze |last3=Raber |first3=Jeffrey C. |title=Determination of Pesticide Residues in Cannabis Smoke |journal=Journal of Toxicology |date=2013 |volume=2013 |article-number=378168 |doi=10.1155/2013/378168 |pmid=23737769 |pmc=3666265 |doi-access=free }}</ref> Marijuana also differs from other agricultural products in that it can not be rinsed with water as the product is traditionally dried or cured.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Caulkins |first1=Jonathan P. |date=Summer 2010 |title=Estimated Cost of Production for Legalized Cannabis |website=Rand |url=https://www.rand.org/pubs/working_papers/WR764.html }}</ref>

The following six pesticides are considered highly toxic but commonly used on marijuana crops:

# [[Myclobutanil]] (fungicide): Developmental and reproductive toxin; Not allowed in WA or CO, found on tested samples in CO and OR
# [[Pyrethrin]] (insecticide): Carcinogen; Approved in CO and WA
# [[Fenoxycarb]] (insecticide): Carcinogen, cholinesterase inhibitor; not allowed in WA
# [[Thiophanate-methyl]] (fungicide): Carcinogen; Not allowed in WA or CO, found on tested samples in CO. 
# [[Avermectin|Avermectin-]] (insecticide): Developmental and reproductive toxin: not allowed in CO or WA, but found on tested samples in CO
# [[Bifenthrin]] (insecticide): Developmental and reproductive toxin, a possible carcinogen; Not allowed in CO or WA, but found on tested samples in CO and OR<ref name=":1" />

Myclobutanil is the active chemical compound in the pesticide Eagle 20EW, the use of which is prohibited in Colorado. However, Eagle 20EW is still a commonly used pesticide. The federal limit, set by the EPA, for the amount in myclobutanil residue on lettuce is 0.3 parts per million – yet the amount tested on marijuana in Denver has at times reached 23.83 parts per million.<ref name=":1" />

A complete list of pesticides allowed for use on cannabis in Colorado approved by the Colorado Department of Agriculture is available {{Deprecated archive|url=https://archive.today/20170301103404/https://www.colorado.gov/pacific/agplants/pesticide-use-cannabis-production-information|title=here}}, and for Washington State, as approved by the Washington State Department of Agriculture is available [https://agr.wa.gov/pestfert/pesticides/pesticideuseonmarijuana.aspx here].

'''Energy Use''': Indoor marijuana cultivation is highly energy-intensive. It is estimated that the industry accounts for 1% of all the nation's electricity use, which is six times the amount the pharmaceutical industry consumes. In terms of emissions, it is estimated that fifteen million metric tons of carbon are produced by the industry annually. Legalization would require those in the industry to meet long-standing statutes such as the Clean Air Act, as well as allow states to enforce provisions on energy use through conditions of licensure. For example, in the city of Boulder, Colorado, marijuana businesses are required to utilize renewable energy to offset 100% of their electricity consumption.<ref>{{cite SSRN |last1=Warren |first1=Gina |title=Regulating Pot to Save the Polar Bear: Energy and Climate Impacts of the Marijuana Industry |date=24 September 2014 |ssrn=2501126}}</ref>

'''Ecosystem:''' A single mature marijuana plant can consume 23 liters of water a day, compared to 13 liters for a grape plant. Historically, many outdoor cultivators have used illegal river and lake diversions to irrigate crops. These diversions have led to the dewatering of streams and rivers which is well documented in areas of Northern California. As with any other agricultural crop, an increase in demand leads to increased clear-cutting of forests which can increase erosion, habitat destruction, and river diversion. Legalization and subsequent regulation could mitigate such issues.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Carah |first1=Jennifer K. |last2=Howard |first2=Jeanette K. |last3=Thompson |first3=Sally E. |last4=Short Gianotti |first4=Anne G. |last5=Bauer |first5=Scott D. |last6=Carlson |first6=Stephanie M. |last7=Dralle |first7=David N. |last8=Gabriel |first8=Mourad W. |last9=Hulette |first9=Lisa L. |last10=Johnson |first10=Brian J. |last11=Knight |first11=Curtis A. |last12=Kupferberg |first12=Sarah J. |last13=Martin |first13=Stefanie L. |last14=Naylor |first14=Rosamond L. |last15=Power |first15=Mary E. |title=High Time for Conservation: Adding the Environment to the Debate on Marijuana Liberalization |journal=BioScience |date=1 August 2015 |volume=65 |issue=8 |pages=822–829 |doi=10.1093/biosci/biv083 |pmid=26955083 |pmc=4776720 }}</ref>

== Arguments in opposition to reform ==

=== Subsequent use of other illicit drugs ===
{{further|Gateway drug effect}}
In 1985, [[Gabriel G. Nahas]] published ''Keep Off the Grass'', which stated that "[the] biochemical changes induced by marijuana in the brain result in drug-seeking, drug-taking behavior, which in many instances will lead the user to experiment with other pleasurable substances. The risk of progression from marijuana to cocaine to heroin is now well documented."<ref>{{cite book|last=Nahas|first=Gabriel|author-link=Gabriel G. Nahas|title=Keep off the Grass|publisher=P.S. Eriksson|year=1985|isbn=978-0-8397-4384-2}}</ref>

In 1995, [[The Partnership for a Drug-Free America|Partnership for a Drug-Free America]] with support from the [[National Institute on Drug Abuse]] (NIDA) and the White House Office of Drug Control Policy launched a campaign against cannabis use citing a [[Center on Addiction and Substance Abuse]] (CASA) report, which claimed that cannabis users are 85 times more likely than non-cannabis users to try cocaine.<ref name=ag>{{cite web|url=http://www.drcnet.org/pubs/guide/06-95/gateway.html|title=Marijuana's Gateway Myth|website=drcnet.org|publisher=The Activist Guide|access-date=2006-03-26}}</ref> Additionally, some research suggests that marijuana use is likely to precede the use of other licit and illicit substances.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.drugabuse.gov/publications/research-reports/marijuana/marijuana-gateway-drug|title=Marijuana|date=June 25, 2018|work=National Institute on Drug Abuse|access-date=May 26, 2019}}</ref> However, an article published in ''The Activist Guide'' by John Morgan and Lynn Zimmer entitled "Marijuana's Gateway Myth", claims CASA's statistic is false. The article states:<ref name="ag" /><!---none of these people NOR the book have articles and are therefore not notable. There must be a better reference someplace. [[WP:NN]] people should NOT be in the article by name since they don't have notability--->

{{blockquote|The high risk-factor obtained is a product not of the fact that so many marijuana users use cocaine but that so many cocaine users used marijuana previously. It is hardly a revelation that people who use one of the least popular drugs are likely to use the more popular ones — not only marijuana, but also alcohol and tobacco cigarettes. The obvious statistic not publicized by CASA is that most marijuana users — 83 percent — never use cocaine.}}

Multiple opponents of cannabis decriminalization have claimed increased cannabis use results in increased use of other illicit drugs.<ref name="msnbc1" /><ref>{{cite news |url= http://www.theindiancatholic.com/newsread.asp?nid=7768 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20070709104943/http://www.theindiancatholic.com/newsread.asp?nid=7768 |url-status= usurped |archive-date= July 9, 2007 |publisher= [[Zenit News Agency]] |website= theindiancatholic.com |date=2007-06-03 |access-date= 2007-06-10|title=Light Drugs, Heavy Consequences}}</ref> However, multiple studies have found no evidence of a correlation between cannabis use and the subsequent use of other illicit drugs.

In 1997, the Connecticut Law Revision Commission examined states that had decriminalized cannabis and found decriminalizing small amounts of cannabis has no effect on subsequent use of alcohol or "harder" illicit drugs. The study recommended [[Connecticut]] reduce cannabis possession of one ounce or less for adults age 21 and over to a civil fine.<ref name="clr">{{cite web|url=http://www.cga.ct.gov/lrc/drugpolicy/drugpolicyrpt2.htm#SecD7|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20050408163928/http://www.cga.ct.gov/lrc/DrugPolicy/DrugPolicyRpt2.htm#SecD7|archive-date=April 8, 2005|title=Connecticut Law Revision Commission Drug Policy Report – Part 1|publisher=Connecticut Law Revision Commission|access-date=2007-03-26|date=1997-01-21}}</ref>

In 1999, a study by the Division of Neuroscience and Behavioral Health at the [[Institute of Medicine]] entitled "Marijuana and Medicine: Assessing the Science Base", found no evidence of a link between cannabis use and the subsequent use of other illicit drugs on the basis of its particular physiological effect.<ref name=IOM>{{cite web|url=http://books.nap.edu/readingroom/books/marimed/notice.html|title=Marijuana and Medicine: Assessing the Science Base|publisher=[[National Academies Press]]|year=1999|access-date=2007-03-30}}</ref>

In December 2002, a study by [[RAND]] investigating whether cannabis use results in the subsequent use of [[cocaine]] and [[heroin]] was published in the British Journal of Addiction. The researchers created a mathematical model simulating adolescent drug use. National rates of cannabis and hard drug use in the model matched survey data collected from representative samples of youths from across the United States; the model produced patterns of substance use. The study stated:<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.rand.org/news/press.02/gateway.html |publisher=[[RAND]] |date=2002-12-02 |access-date=2007-06-10 |title=RAND Study Casts Doubt on Claims that Marijuana Acts as "Gateway" to the Use of Cocaine and Heroin. |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20061104124529/https://www.rand.org/news/press.02/gateway.html |archive-date=2006-11-04 }}</ref>

{{blockquote|The people who are predisposed to use drugs and have the opportunity to use drugs are more likely than others to use both marijuana and harder drugs ... Marijuana typically comes first because it is more available. Once we incorporated these facts into our mathematical model of adolescent drug use, we could explain all of the drug use associations that have been cited as evidence of marijuana's gateway effect ... We've shown that the marijuana gateway effect is not the best explanation for the link between marijuana use and the use of harder drugs.}}

In 2004, a study by Craig Reinarman, Peter D. A. Cohen, and Hendrien L. Kaal entitled "The Limited Relevance of Drug Policy: Cannabis in Amsterdam and in San Francisco", was published in the [[American Journal of Public Health]]. The study found no evidence that the decriminalization of cannabis leads to subsequent use of other illicit drugs. The study also found the mean age at onset of cannabis use and the mean age of cannabis users are both higher in Amsterdam than in San Francisco.<ref>{{cite news|author=Jennifer McNulty|title=Dutch drug policies do not increase marijuana use, first rigorous comparative study finds|url=http://www.ucsc.edu/currents/03-04/05-03/drug_study.html|website=currents.ucsc.edu|publisher=UC Santa Cruz Currants|date=2004-05-03|access-date=2007-03-20|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070311002157/http://www.ucsc.edu/currents/03-04/05-03/drug_study.html|archive-date=2007-03-11}}</ref><ref name="ajph">{{cite journal |last1=Reinarman |first1=Craig |last2=Cohen |first2=Peter D. A. |last3=Kaal |first3=Hendrien L. |title=The Limited Relevance of Drug Policy: Cannabis in Amsterdam and in San Francisco |journal=American Journal of Public Health |date=May 2004 |volume=94 |issue=5 |pages=836–842 |doi=10.2105/ajph.94.5.836 |pmid=15117709 |pmc=1448346 }}</ref>

In 2006, the [[Karolinska Institute]] in Sweden used twelve rats to examine how adolescent use of cannabis affects subsequent use of other illicit drugs. The study gave six of the twelve "teenage" rats a small dose of [[Tetrahydrocannabinol|THC]], reportedly equivalent to one joint smoked by a human, every three days. The rats were allowed to administer [[heroin]] by pushing a lever and the study found the rats given THC took larger doses of heroin. The institute examined the brain cells in the rats and found THC alters the opioid system that is associated with positive emotions, which lessens the effects of [[opiate]]s on rat's brain and thus causes them to use more heroin.<ref>{{cite magazine|title=Why teenagers should steer clear of cannabis|date=2006-07-05|access-date=2007-05-12|last=Vince|first=Gaia|magazine=New Scientist|url=https://www.newscientist.com/article.ns?id=dn9488}}</ref> Paul Armentano, policy analyst for [[National Organization for the Reform of Marijuana Laws (United States)|NORML]], claimed because the rats were given THC at the young age of 28 days, it is impossible to extrapolate the results of this study to humans.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.austinchronicle.com/gyrobase/Issue/column?oid=oid%3A416164|title=Reefer Madness|first=Jordan|last=Smith|date=2006-11-03|access-date=2007-05-13|newspaper=The Austin Chronicle}}</ref>

In December 2006, a 12-year gateway drug hypothesis study on 214 boys from ages 10–12 by the [[American Psychiatric Association]] was published in the [[American Journal of Psychiatry]]. The study concluded adolescents who used cannabis prior to using other drugs, including alcohol and tobacco, were no more likely to develop a [[substance use disorder]] than subjects in the study who did not use cannabis prior to using other drugs.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.norml.org/index.cfm?Group_ID=7118|publisher=[[National Organization for the Reform of Marijuana Laws (United States)|NORML]]|date=2006-12-07|title=Marijuana Use Per Se Not a 'Gateway' To Illicit Drug Use, Study Says|access-date=2007-06-09|archive-date=March 13, 2007|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070313174544/http://www.norml.org/index.cfm?Group_ID=7118}}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal|url=http://ajp.psychiatryonline.org/cgi/content/abstract/163/12/2134|access-date= 2007-06-09|title=Predictors of Marijuana Use in Adolescents Before and After Licit Drug Use: Examination of the Gateway Hypothesis|journal=American Journal of Psychiatry|volume = 163|issue= 12|pages= 2134–2140|date=December 2006|doi=10.1176/ajp.2006.163.12.2134|pmid= 17151165|last1=Tarter|first1=Ralph E.|last2=Vanyukov|first2=Michael|last3=Kirisci|first3=Levent|last4=Reynolds|first4=Maureen|last5=Clark|first5=Duncan B.|url-access=subscription}}</ref>

In September 2010, a study from the [[University of New Hampshire]] examined survey data from 1,286 young adults who had attended [[Miami-Dade County Public Schools]] in the 1990s and found the association between teenage cannabis use and other illicit drug use by young adults was significantly diminished after controlling for other factors, such as unemployment. They found that after young adults reach age 21, the gateway effect subsides entirely.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.sciencedaily.com/releases/2010/09/100902073507.htm|website=[[Science Daily]]|date=2010-09-02|title=Risk of Marijuana's 'Gateway Effect' Overblown, New Research Shows|access-date=2010-12-24}}</ref>

=== Increased crime ===
The [[Drug Enforcement Administration]] (DEA) has claimed that cannabis leads to increased crime in the pamphlet entitled "Speaking Out Against Drug Legalization"<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.usdoj.gov/dea/demand/speakout/09so.htm|title=Fact 9: Europe's More Liberal Drug Policies Are Not the Right Model for America|publisher=[[Drug Enforcement Administration]]|access-date=2007-04-17|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070210122959/http://www.usdoj.gov/dea/demand/speakout/09so.htm|archive-date=2007-02-10}}</ref>

In 2001, a report by David Boyum and [[Mark A.R. Kleiman]] entitled "Substance Abuse Policy from a Crime-Control Perspective" found the "high" from cannabis is unlikely to trigger violence and concluded:<ref>{{cite web|url=http://ucla.edu/faculty/kleiman/subabus.pdf|title=Substance Abuse Policy from a Crime-Control Perspective|author1=Boyum, David A.|author2=Mark A.R. Kleiman|year=2001|access-date=2007-06-07 |archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20150513041705/http://www.ucla.edu/faculty/kleiman/subabus.pdf|archive-date=2015-05-13 }}{{Verify source|type=archive|date=May 2019}}</ref>

{{blockquote|Making marijuana legally available to adults on more or less the same terms as alcohol would tend to reduce crime, certainly by greatly shrinking the illicit market and possibly by reducing alcohol consumption via substitution if smoking marijuana acts, on balance, as a substitute for drinking alcohol rather than a complement to it since drinking seems to have a greater tendency to unleash aggression than does cannabis use.}}

In 2004, a study by Scott Bates from the Boreal Economic Analysis & Research center entitled "The Economic Implications of Marijuana Legalization in Alaska", was prepared for Alaskans for Rights & Revenues. The study found there was no link between cannabis use and criminal behavior.<ref name="prohibcosts" />

A 2014 study published in [[PLoS ONE]] found that not only did the legalization of [[medical cannabis]] not increase violent crime, but that a 2.4% reduction in homicide and assault was found for each year the law was in effect.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Morris |first1=Robert G. |last2=TenEyck |first2=Michael |last3=Barnes |first3=J. C. |last4=Kovandzic |first4=Tomislav V. |last5=Keating |first5=Joseph A. |title=The Effect of Medical Marijuana Laws on Crime: Evidence from State Panel Data, 1990–2006 |journal=PLOS ONE |date=26 March 2014 |volume=9 |issue=3 |article-number=e92816 |doi=10.1371/journal.pone.0092816 |pmid=24671103 |pmc=3966811 |bibcode=2014PLoSO...992816M |doi-access=free }}</ref>

=== Increased cannabis usage ===
The [[Drug Enforcement Administration]] (DEA) has claimed that cannabis decriminalization will lead to increased cannabis use and addiction in the un-sourced pamphlet entitled "Speaking Out Against Drug Legalization".<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.usdoj.gov/dea/demand/speakout/06so.htm|title=Speaking Out Against Drug Legalization, Fact 6|date=May 2006|publisher=[[Drug Enforcement Administration]]|access-date=2007-03-26|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070210094634/http://www.usdoj.gov/dea/demand/speakout/06so.htm|archive-date=2007-02-10}}</ref> The pamphlet states in 1979, after 11 states decriminalized private cannabis use, cannabis use among 12th grade students was almost 51 percent and in 1992, when stricter cannabis laws were put in place, the usage rate reduced to 22 percent. The pamphlet also states that when Alaska decriminalized cannabis in 1975, the cannabis use rate among youth eventually rose to twice the national average youth usage rate nationwide; even though the law did not apply to anyone under the age of 19, the pamphlet explains this is why Alaska re-criminalized cannabis in 1990. Save Our Society From Drugs (SOS) has also stated that decriminalizing cannabis will increase usage among teenagers, citing an increase in Alaskan youth cannabis usage when cannabis was decriminalized.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://cbs4denver.com/local/local_story_263172020.html|title=Opponents Take Aim At Marijuana Amendment|publisher=[[CBS]]: Denver|date=2006-09-20|access-date=2007-04-19 |archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20060920215631/http://cbs4denver.com/local/local_story_263172020.html |archive-date = 2006-09-20}}</ref>  However, cannabis use rose in all states in the 1970s, and the DEA does not say whether or not Alaska started out higher than the national average. Following decriminalization, Alaska youth had lower rates of daily use of cannabis than their peers in the rest of the US.<ref name="cedro-uva.org">{{cite web|url=http://www.cedro-uva.org/lib/harrison.cannabis.06.html |title=Marijuana policy and prevalence |publisher=Cedro-uva.org |access-date=2010-09-15}}</ref>

In 1972, President [[Richard Nixon]] commissioned the [[National Commission on Marijuana and Drug Abuse]] to produce an in-depth report on cannabis. The report, entitled "Marijuana: A Signal of Misunderstanding", reviewed existing cannabis studies and concluded that cannabis does not cause physical addiction.<ref name=nixonreport />

Studies conducted in Oregon, California, and Maine within a few years of decriminalization found little increase in cannabis use, compared to the rest of the country; "The most frequently cited reasons for non-use by respondents was 'not interested,' cited by about 80% of non-users. Only 4% of adults indicated fear of arrest and prosecution or unavailability as factors preventing use."<ref name="cedro-uva.org"/>

In 1997, the Connecticut Law Revision Commission examined states that had decriminalized cannabis and found an increase in cannabis usage was less than the increase in states that have not decriminalized cannabis; furthermore, the commission stated: "the largest proportionate increase [of cannabis use] occurred in those states with the most severe penalties." The study recommended [[Connecticut]] reduce cannabis possession of 28.35 grams (one ounce) or less for adults age 21 and over to a civil fine.<ref name="clr" />

In 1999, a study by the Division of Neuroscience and Behavioral Health at the [[Institute of Medicine]] entitled "Marijuana and Medicine: Assessing the Science Base", concluded "there is little evidence that decriminalization of marijuana use necessarily leads to a substantial increase in marijuana use."<ref name=IOM/><!--REF: pg102-->

In 2001, a report by [[Robert MacCoun]] and [[Peter Reuter]] entitled "Evaluating alternative cannabis regimes", was published in the [[British Journal of Psychiatry]]. The report found there was no available evidence cannabis use would increase if cannabis were decriminalized.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://bjp.rcpsych.org/cgi/reprint/178/2/123.pdf|title=Evaluating alternative cannabis regimes|website=bjp.rcpsych.org |publisher=British Journal of Psychiatry|year=2001|access-date=2007-03-31}}</ref>

In 2004, a study entitled "The Limited Relevance of Drug Policy: Cannabis in Amsterdam and in San Francisco", found strict laws against cannabis use have a low impact on usage rates.<ref name="ajph" />

=== Increased safety concerns ===
Studies conducted following the legalization of cannabis in Washington and Colorado show that driving under the effects of marijuana increases a driver's likelihood of getting in accident by 100% in comparison to sober drivers. They also suggest that increased use will lead to higher workplace accidents, with employees who tested positive for cannabis being 55% more likely to get in an accident, and 85% more likely to get injured on the job.<ref>[http://search.ebscohost.com/login.aspx?direct=true&db=a9h&AN=124424502&site=ehost-live "Public Health and Safety Issues Remain."] ''USA Today Magazine'', vol. 146, no. 2867, Aug. 2017, p. 12. EBSCO''host''.</ref>

===Big business===
In a ''[[Huffington Post]]'' interview, [[Mark A.R. Kleiman|Mark Kleiman]], the "Pot [[Czar (political term)|Czar]]" of [[Washington (state)|Washington state]], said he was concerned that the [[National Cannabis Industry Association]] would favor profits over public health. He also said that it could become a predatory body like the lobbying arms of the tobacco and alcohol industries. Kleiman said: "The fact that the National Cannabis Industry Association has hired itself a [[K Street (Washington, D.C.)|K Street]] [[Lobbying in the United States|suit [lobbyist]]] is not a good sign."<ref name=huffpo2>{{cite news| url=http://www.huffingtonpost.com/2014/04/18/weed-legalization_n_5169750.html | work=Huffington Post | title=7 Things That Could Totally Kill Weed Legalization's Buzz | date=18 April 2014}}</ref>

In an episode of the [[YouTube]] and [[Netflix]] show ''[[Patriot Act with Hasan Minhaj#Volume 6 (2020)|Patriot Act with Hasan Minhaj]]'', comedian and political commentator [[Hasan Minhaj]] laid out the ways by which the new marijuana industry has been rigged to favor the interests of big business entrepreneurs such as [[John Boehner]] and his [[Acreage Holdings]].<ref>{{Citation|title=The Legal Marijuana Industry Is Rigged {{!}} Patriot Act with Hasan Minhaj {{!}} Netflix| date=May 25, 2020 |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=JP3416CgU0Q|language=en|access-date=2021-09-23}}</ref>

== Advocacy ==
Several U.S.-based advocate groups seek to modify the [[drug policy of the United States]] to decriminalize cannabis. These groups include [[Law Enforcement Against Prohibition]], [[Students for Sensible Drug Policy]], [[Drug Policy Alliance|The Drug Policy Alliance]], the [[Marijuana Policy Project]], [[NORML]], [[Coalition for Rescheduling Cannabis]], and [[Americans for Safe Access]]. There are also many individual American cannabis activists, such as [[Jack Herer]], [[Paul Armentano]], [[Edward Forchion]], [[Jon Gettman]], [[Rob Kampia]], and [[Keith Stroup]]; [[Marc Emery]], a well-known Canadian activist, has supported cannabis activism in the U.S. among other countries by donating money earned from ''[[Cannabis Culture magazine|Cannabis Culture]]'' magazine and Emeryseeds.com.

In 1997, the Connecticut Law Revision Commission recommended [[Connecticut]] reduce cannabis possession of one ounce or less for adults age 21 and over to a civil fine.<ref name=clr /> In 2001, the [[New Mexico]] state-commissioned Drug Policy Advisory Group stated that decriminalizing cannabis "will result in greater availability of resources to respond to more serious crimes without any increased risks to public safety."<ref name=nm />

A few places in [[California]] had been advocating cannabis decriminalization.
On November 3, 2004, [[Oakland, California|Oakland]] passed Proposition Z, which makes "adult recreational marijuana use, cultivation and sales the lowest [city] law enforcement priority."<ref>{{cite web |url = http://journalism.berkeley.edu/projects/election2004/archives/2004/11/proposition_z_w.html |title = Election 2004: Oakland Marijuana Measure in the Bag |publisher = journalism.berkeley.edu |access-date = 2008-07-07 |archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20080605031828/http://journalism.berkeley.edu/projects/election2004/archives/2004/11/proposition_z_w.html |archive-date = 2008-06-05 }}</ref>  The proposition states the city of Oakland must advocate to the state of California to adopt laws to regulate and tax cannabis.<ref name="oakland_prop_z">{{cite web|url= http://www.smartvoter.org/2004/11/02/ca/alm/meas/Z/ |website=smartvoter.org|publisher=Smart Voter|title=Measure Z: Marijuana Law Enforcement – Alameda County, CA|access-date=2006-12-24}}</ref> On November 7, 2006, [[Santa Cruz, California|Santa Cruz]] passed [[Measure K 2006|Measure K]], which made cannabis the lowest priority for city law enforcement. The measure requests the Santa Cruz City Clerk send letters annually to [[U.S. state|state]] and [[Federal government of the United States|federal]] representatives advocating reform of cannabis laws.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.sensiblesantacruz.org/initiative.htm |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070106172530/http://www.sensiblesantacruz.org/initiative.htm |archive-date=2007-01-06 |title=Measure K – Lowest Law Enforcement Priority Initiative: FAQ |website=sensiblesantacruz.org |publisher=Santa Cruz Citizens for Sensible Marijuana Policy |access-date=2007-06-11 }}</ref> On June 5, 2007, [[Mendocino County, California|Mendocino County]] Board of Supervisors voted 4–1 to send a letter in support of the legalization, regulation, and taxation of cannabis to [[U.S. state|state]] and [[Federal government of the United States|federal]] legislators, and the [[President of United States]].<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.ukiahdailyjournal.com/ci_6074058|title=Marijuana legalization letter OK'd|first=Katie|last=Mintz|date=2007-06-06|access-date=2007-06-11|publisher=Ukiah Daily Journal|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070615181049/http://ukiahdailyjournal.com/ci_6074058|archive-date=2007-06-15}}</ref>

[[Ron Paul]], a former Texas Congressman and 2008 Presidential Candidate, stated at a rally in response to a question by a medical cannabis patient that he would "never use the federal government to force the law against anybody using marijuana."<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AegRynzxQJA| archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/varchive/youtube/20211117/AegRynzxQJA| archive-date=2021-11-17 | url-status=live|title=Ron Paul on marijuana laws| date=January 2, 2008|publisher=[[YouTube]]|access-date=2008-01-02}}{{cbignore}}</ref>  In his book, ''[[The Revolution: A Manifesto]]'' he writes, "Regardless of where one stands on the broader drug war, we should all be able to agree on the subject of medical marijuana. Here, the use of an otherwise prohibited substance has been found to relieve unbearable suffering in countless patients. How can we fail to support liberty and individual responsibility in such a clear cut case? What harm does it do to anyone else to allow fellow human beings in pain to find the relief they need?"<ref>{{cite book|title=The Revolution: A Manifesto|year=2008|url=https://archive.org/details/revolutionmanife00paul|url-access=registration|publisher=Grand Central Publishing|isbn=978-0-446-53751-3}}</ref>  He is also the cosponsor of the [[Personal Use of Marijuana by Responsible Adults Act of 2008]].

[[Mike Gravel]], a former [[United States Senate|U.S. senator]] from Alaska and [[Mike Gravel presidential campaign, 2008|2008 presidential candidate]], responded to a caller on a [[C-SPAN]] program asking about cannabis and the drug war, he stated "That one is real simple, I would legalize marijuana. You should be able to buy that at a liquor store."<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1KyFRyxZp-s| archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070928233851/http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1KyFRyxZp-s| archive-date=2007-09-28 |title=Part 2 – Mike Gravel – The Issues that Matter – Washington Journal.|publisher=[[YouTube]]|access-date=2007-06-13}}</ref>

[[Dennis Kucinich]], a [[United States House of Representatives|U.S. representative]] from [[Ohio]] and [[Dennis Kucinich#2008 Presidential campaign|2008 presidential candidate]], has been an advocate of cannabis legalization. During Kucinich's 2004 presidential campaign, the following was posted on Kucinich's official campaign web site.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://stopthedrugwar.org/chronicle-old/316/kucinich.shtml|title=Newsbrief: Campaign Watch – Kucinich Says Legalize It|date=2003-12-19|access-date=2007-06-13|website=stopthedrugwar.org}}</ref>
{{blockquote|Most marijuana users do so responsibly, in a safe, recreational context. These people lead normal, productive lives — pursuing careers, raising families and participating in civic life ... A Kucinich administration would reject the current paradigm of 'all use is abuse' in favor of a drug policy that sets reasonable boundaries for marijuana use by establishing guidelines similar to those already in place for alcohol.}}

The issue of marijuana legalization has come up in the [[2024 United States presidential election]]. [[Robert F. Kennedy Jr. 2024 presidential campaign|Independent 2024 presidential candidate]] and [[environmental lawyer]] [[Robert F. Kennedy Jr.]] has supported legalizing marijuana as a method to combat [[drug addiction]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=2023-11-03 |title=RFK Jr. urges marijuana legalization 'to end addiction' |url=https://www.newsnationnow.com/politics/2024-election/rfk-jr-urges-marijuana-legalizatio/ |access-date=2024-04-21 |website=NewsNation |language=en-US}}</ref> [[Ron DeSantis 2024 presidential campaign|Republican presidential candidate]] and [[Florida Governor]] [[Ron DeSantis]], however, said he "would not legalize" marijuana as [[President of the United States]], claiming that marijuana is "killing this country".<ref>{{Cite web |last=Gancarski |first=A. G. |date=2023-08-27 |title=Ron DeSantis says he 'would not legalize' marijuana amid worries about 'kids' and 'fentanyl' |url=https://floridapolitics.com/archives/631088-ron-desantis-marijuana/ |access-date=2024-04-21 |website=[[Florida Politics]] |language=en-US}}</ref> [[Nikki Haley]], another [[Nikki Haley 2024 presidential campaign|Republican presidential candidate]] and former [[United States Ambassador to the United Nations|U.S. Ambassador to the U.N.]], insisted that marijuana legalization is a [[states' rights]] issue.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Sexton |first=Adam |date=2023-05-26 |title=Conversation with the Candidate: Nikki Haley calls for cognitive test for president |url=https://www.wmur.com/article/nikki-haley-cognitive-tests-marijuana/43919440 |access-date=2024-04-21 |website=WMUR |language=en}}</ref>

Some members of religious organizations, even while not necessarily being in favor of marijuana consumption, have also spoken in favor of reform, for medical reasons, or the social costs of enforcement and incarceration.<ref name="religionnews">{{cite web|url=http://www.religionnews.com/2014/02/03/faith-leaders-wrestle-growing-support-marijuana/ |title=Faith leaders wrestle over growing support for marijuana |publisher=Religion News Service |date=2014-02-03 |author=Sarah Pulliam Bailey |access-date=2014-05-04}}</ref>  For instance, Revered Samuel Rodriguez of National Hispanic Christian Leadership Conferences stated that "laws that prohibit marijuana affect the minorities significantly and hence should be reconsidered." Religious groups uphold that marijuana does not harm as much as alcohol does and thus legalizing it for medicinal usage would not be harmful to the economy.<ref name="religionnews" />

In 1974 Dr [[Robert DuPont]] began to publicly support decriminalization of cannabis, seeing cannabis as a health problem. But when DuPont left government he changed his mind and declared that "decriminalization is a bad idea".<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.pbs.org/wgbh/pages/frontline/shows/drugs/interviews/dupont.html |title=WGBH educational foundation. Interview with Dr. Robert Dupont |publisher=PBS |date=1970-02-18 |access-date=2011-04-20}}</ref> Robert DuPont is still an active opponent of decriminalization of cannabis.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.cnbc.com/id/36267223/Why_We_Should_Not_Legalize_Marijuana |title=Robert DuPont: Why We Should Not Legalize Marijuana, CNBS, 20 Apr 2010 |publisher=CNBC |date=2010-04-20 |access-date=2014-05-03 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140715235629/https://www.cnbc.com/id/36267223/Why_We_Should_Not_Legalize_Marijuana |archive-date=2014-07-15 }}</ref>

